  { id:'opqb-t8-297', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Band shaped keratopathy** is commonly caused by deposition of:',
    options:['Magnesium salt','Calcium salt','Ferrous salt','Copper salt'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Calcific Band Keratopathy:** It is a corneal degeneration that is composed of fine dust-like **calcium deposits** in the sub-epithelium, Bowman’s layer, and the anterior stroma.\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The deck says the same in one line and adds the layer and the zone.** `L15,16) Cornea`, corneal degenerations: \"**Band keratopathy: calcium deposits in Bowman’s layer in interpalpebral zone**; associated with chronic ocular disease, hypercalcaemia, chronic uveitis.\" The *where* is asked separately by this bank at `opqb-t8-322`.\n\n**The causes, as the deck's own side panel lists them:** chronic anterior uveitis · **hypercalcaemia** · silicone oil in the anterior chamber · end-stage ocular disease · gout (hyperuricaemia) · familial/hereditary · idiopathic age-related. Two of those are systemic, which is why a band keratopathy in a young patient is a reason to check a serum calcium.\n\n**Treatment**, also printed: \"**EDTA chelation** (effective) or **excimer laser PTK**\" — chelation because the deposit is a calcium salt, and a chelator is what lifts a calcium salt off Bowman's layer.\n\n**Why the other three metals are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What that metal actually deposits in the eye |\n|---|---|\n| **Magnesium salt** | Nothing. No magnesium deposition disease of the cornea is taught anywhere in the module, and none exists in practice. A pure filler. |\n| **Ferrous salt** | Iron does deposit in the cornea, but as **lines**, not a band — the Fleischer ring at the base of the cone in keratoconus, the Hudson-Stähli line, the Stocker line at a pterygium head. Iron also makes the **rust ring** of a retained metallic foreign body, which the deck does teach: \"Rust ring: after iron FB; remove at 24 h with burr\". Wrong pattern, wrong place. |\n| **Copper salt** | Copper gives the **Kayser-Fleischer ring** of Wilson's disease — a ring in **Descemet's membrane at the limbus**, not a band in Bowman's layer across the middle. This bank offers that same sign as a distractor at `opqb-t8-313`. |\n\n*(The Fleischer ring, Hudson-Stähli line and Stocker line are **not taken from the course material** — `L15,16)` names no corneal iron line, and *Fleischer* returns zero hits across all cached ophthalmology decks and book chapters. The Kayser-Fleischer ring is likewise absent from every cached source; it appears in this module only as a printed distractor in this bank. They are given because the question is built out of metals.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A horizontal grey-white band across the exposed strip of cornea, densest at 3 and 9 o'clock and often with clear lucencies where nerves pierce Bowman's layer, is calcium. Ask what has been inflaming or failing in that eye for years — and check the calcium.",
    objective:'Band keratopathy is CALCIUM deposited in Bowman’s layer across the interpalpebral strip; iron makes corneal lines and copper makes the Kayser-Fleischer ring.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.46 (Q297), key p.52 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-298', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Corneal sensations are diminished** in:',
    options:['Herpes simplex','Conjunctivitis','Fungal infections','Marginal keratitis'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"HSV can cause **neurotrophic keratitis** → **decreased corneal sensation due to trigeminal nerve involvement.**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The cornea is the most densely innervated tissue in the body, and every one of those nerves is a branch of V1** — `L15,16) Cornea` opens with exactly that: \"Most densely innervated tissue in the body (**trigeminal nerve, ophthalmic division**)\", \"Innervation: **CN V1 (ophthalmic)**\". Anything that damages those nerves anaesthetises the cornea, and the deck names the culprits under **neurotrophic keratopathy**: \"trigeminal anaesthesia → epithelial breakdown → persistent epithelial defect → melting\", with \"Causes of neurotrophic: **HSV, HZV**, acoustic neuroma surgery, DM, topical anaesthetic abuse\".\n\nHerpes simplex heads that list, and this bank keys corneal hypoesthesia as a defining feature of herpes simplex keratitis again at `opqb-t8-304`.\n\n**Why the other three do not reduce sensation:**\n\n| Option | What it does instead |\n|---|---|\n| **Conjunctivitis** | Does not touch the cornea's nerves at all — it is a conjunctival disease. Sensation is normal, or if anything the eye is *more* uncomfortable. |\n| **Fungal infections** | An infiltrative stromal disease with \"feathery/satellite lesions, dry rough surface, hypopyon\" (`L15,16)`). It hurts; it does not anaesthetise. It is the answer to `opqb-t8-310` instead. |\n| **Marginal keratitis** | \"Immune-mediated reaction to **Staphylococcal antigens**; peripheral subepithelial infiltrates with clear limbal zone\" (`L15,16)`). Peripheral, immune, and sensate. |\n\n**The clinical consequence, which is why the fact is worth more than a mark.** A cornea that cannot feel cannot protect itself: it does not blink enough, it does not water, and the patient does not complain — so the epithelium breaks down painlessly and can melt to perforation while the eye looks quiet. That is why the deck's treatment list for neurotrophic keratopathy is entirely protective — \"preservative-free lubrication, bandage lens, tarsorrhaphy, **cenegermin** (nerve growth factor) eye drops\" — and why the same deck bans topical anaesthetics outright (`opqb-t8-348`): abusing them produces the very anaesthesia that destroys the cornea.\n\n**Test it before you put in the drops.** A wisp of cotton wool touched to the cornea from the side, comparing the two eyes, takes seconds — and anaesthetic drops abolish the finding you were about to look for.",
    objective:'Corneal sensation is V1; herpes simplex (and zoster) damage it and cause neurotrophic keratopathy, in which a painless cornea breaks down and can melt.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.46 (Q298), key p.52 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-299', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The **color of fluorescein staining** in the corneal ulcer is:',
    options:['Yellow','Blue','Green','Royal blue'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Fluorescein is an orange-yellow dye that FLUORESCES green when it is excited by blue light — so the ulcer looks green and the light you shine on it is blue.** The two are constantly swapped, and that is exactly what options B and D are for.\n\n| | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **The dye in the bottle / on the strip** | Orange-yellow |\n| **The illumination you use** | **Cobalt blue** filter on the slit lamp or the blue light of a pen torch |\n| **What you see at the defect** | **Bright green** |\n\n**Why an ulcer takes the stain at all.** `L15,16) Cornea` gives the mechanism in its layers slide: the epithelium has \"Tight junctions → **barrier function**\" and \"**Stains with fluorescein when disrupted**\". Intact epithelium is watertight and repels the dye; wherever the epithelium is missing the dye soaks into the exposed hydrophilic stroma and pools. So fluorescein does not stain an ulcer — it stains the **absence of epithelium**, which is why this bank calls a positive test the \"sure diagnostic sign\" at `opqb-t8-302`, and why it is useless for an intact-epithelium lesion such as a scar (`opqb-t8-339`).\n\n**Where the deck uses it:** \"Fluorescein: **stains the ulcer bed**\" in herpes simplex epithelial keratitis, against \"**Rose Bengal**: stains virus-laden margins\" — two dyes, two targets, and the pair is worth holding together. And in trauma: \"Corneal abrasion: **fluorescein staining delineates defect**\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why |\n|---|---|\n| **Yellow** | The colour of the **dye itself**, undiluted on the strip or in a thick tear meniscus under white light. Not the colour of the stained lesion under the filter. The commonest wrong answer. |\n| **Blue** | The colour of the **exciting light**, not of the emission. |\n| **Royal blue** | The same confusion said more precisely — cobalt blue is the filter's name. Still the lamp, not the lesion. |\n\n*(The excitation/emission physics — fluorescein absorbs around blue and emits around green — is **not taken from the course material**; the deck states only that disrupted epithelium stains. It is standard and uncontested.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** If you look at a suspected ulcer under white light and see nothing, you have not done the test. Put the strip in, ask the patient to blink, switch to cobalt blue, and look for green.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Fluorescein is an orange-yellow dye viewed under COBALT BLUE light; the epithelial defect fluoresces GREEN, because only bare stroma takes up the dye.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.46 (Q299), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-300', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'Which of the following organisms can **penetrate intact corneal epithelium**?',
    options:['Strept pyogenes','Staph aureus','Pseudomonas pyocyanaea','Corynebacterium diphtheriae'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ A DECK-vs-BANK KEY DIVERGENCE, RECORDED AND NOT CORRECTED. The key is C, Pseudomonas, as printed. The course material names a DIFFERENT organism for this property and does not name Pseudomonas.** `L15,16) Cornea`, bacterial keratitis, key organisms, lists them one under the other:\n\n> Gram-positive: *Staphylococcus aureus, S. pneumoniae, Streptococcus pyogenes*\n> Gram-negative: ***Pseudomonas aeruginosa* (contact lens – rapid, aggressive)**\n> ***Neisseria gonorrhoeae* (can penetrate intact epithelium)**\n> *Moraxella*: elderly, debilitated patients\n> Atypical mycobacteria: post-refractive surgery\n\nThe parenthesis \"(can penetrate intact epithelium)\" is attached to ***Neisseria gonorrhoeae* and to nothing else** in the deck, and *N. gonorrhoeae* is **not among this question's four options**. Pseudomonas gets a different parenthesis — \"contact lens – rapid, aggressive\" — and the deck's severity line, \"**Pseudomonas can cause rapid descemetocele → perforation within 24–48 h**\". Measured, not asserted: the phrase *intact epithelium* returns exactly one hit across all 29 cached ophthalmology decks and 12 book chapters, and that hit is in `book/ch06-conjunctiva.txt`, describing a **pseudomembrane** lying on \"the surface of the inflamed intact epithelium\" — a different sense of the words entirely, and nothing to do with invasion.\n\n**So how is the key defensible?** Not from this module's cache, but from the standard teaching that a short list of organisms can invade an unbroken cornea — classically *Neisseria gonorrhoeae*, *Neisseria meningitidis*, *Corynebacterium diphtheriae*, *Haemophilus aegyptius* (Koch-Weeks) and *Listeria*. **On that classical list, the option here that belongs is D, *Corynebacterium diphtheriae*, not C.** *(That list is **not taken from the course material** and is flagged as outside knowledge.)* Pseudomonas is instead the organism that exploits the **tiniest** break — a contact-lens micro-abrasion — and then digests the stroma with proteases fast enough to perforate in two days, which is very likely the reputation the bank is keying.\n\n**Answer C, because the bank prints C.** The project's rule is that a defective or contestable key is recorded, never disputed. Sit the question, key C; know that if an examiner asks the same thing from a microbiology angle the expected answer may be diphtheria or gonococcus.\n\n**Why the other three are offered:**\n\n| Option | What the deck actually says about it |\n|---|---|\n| **Strept pyogenes** | Listed as a Gram-positive cause of bacterial keratitis, with no invasive property attached. Needs a break. |\n| **Staph aureus** | Same — and it is also the antigen source behind **marginal keratitis** (`L15,16)`: \"immune-mediated reaction to Staphylococcal antigens\"). Needs a break. |\n| **Corynebacterium diphtheriae** | Cause of **membranous conjunctivitis** in book ch.6 (\"Causes: *Corynebacterium diphtheriae* [membranous] conjunctivitis\"). See above: on the classical list this is the strongest of the four, and it is not the key. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The practical version of this fact is a rule about referral speed: a purulent conjunctivitis that is **hyperacute** — massive discharge in a day or two, especially a neonate or a sexually active adult — is gonococcal until proved otherwise and threatens the cornea **without any injury having occurred**. That eye needs systemic treatment the same day.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'This bank keys Pseudomonas, but the deck attaches "can penetrate intact epithelium" to Neisseria gonorrhoeae alone — record the key, know the divergence, and remember Pseudomonas for its 24–48 h perforation instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.46 (Q300), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-301', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Topical steroids are contraindicated** in a case of **viral** corneal ulcer for fear of:',
    options:['Secondary glaucoma','Cortical cataract.','Corneal perforation','Secondary viral infection'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A steroid dropped onto an epithelial herpetic ulcer lets the virus replicate freely and stops the stroma from repairing itself, and the end of that road is a hole in the cornea.** `L15,16) Cornea` prints it twice, both times in a warning box. First as a CLINICAL PEARL: \"**NEVER use topical steroids in EPITHELIAL herpes keratitis** – this promotes viral replication and can result in **corneal perforation**.\" Then again in its *Common Prescribing Errors* table, under *Topical Steroids in Epithelial HSV*: \"Promotes viral replication · Can cause **geographic ulcer** · **Risk of perforation** · Only use in STROMAL / disciform stage with antiviral cover.\"\n\n**The sequence is worth holding as a chain, because each link is a printed phrase:** steroid → viral replication unchecked → the branching **dendrite** enlarges into a **geographic** ulcer → stromal melting → **perforation**.\n\n**Where steroids ARE correct in herpes**, so the rule is not over-applied: **disciform (stromal immune) keratitis**, which the deck defines as \"Immune-mediated endotheliitis · Central corneal disc-like oedema · Keratic precipitates under area of oedema · **Treat with TOPICAL STEROIDS + antivirals**\". The distinction is epithelial (virus replicating — no steroid) versus stromal immune (immune damage — steroid, always under antiviral cover).\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it is not the fear HERE |\n|---|---|\n| **Secondary glaucoma** | A real hazard of prolonged topical steroids — the steroid-responder rise in IOP — but it is a **chronic** complication of steroid use in any eye, not the specific danger of steroid in a viral ulcer, and it will not blind the patient this week. |\n| **Cortical cataract.** | Steroid cataract is real but it is **posterior subcapsular**, not cortical — and again it is a long-term effect of prolonged use, not the reason to withhold the drop today. Doubly wrong: wrong morphology and wrong timescale. |\n| **Secondary viral infection** | Reads plausibly but is the wrong word. The infection is not *secondary* — the virus is already there and is the primary disease. What the steroid does is **worsen the existing** infection. |\n\n**⚠️ Note the sibling.** `opqb-t8-326` asks the identical question about a **bacterial** ulcer, offers the same four options, and has the same key. The deck's reasoning differs — for bacteria: \"Can potentiate infection · Delay healing · Wait until infection is controlled\" — but the feared endpoint is the same, and the deck's general rule under bacterial keratitis treatment is \"**AVOID topical steroids until infectious cause is excluded or controlled**\".\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The single most dangerous prescription in general practice ophthalmology is a steroid drop given for a red eye that was never stained with fluorescein. Stain first. If it stains as a branching ulcer, the drop is an antiviral, never a steroid.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Steroids in EPITHELIAL herpetic keratitis promote viral replication, turn a dendrite into a geographic ulcer and risk perforation; they belong only in the stromal/disciform stage, with antiviral cover.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.46 (Q301), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-302', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The **sure diagnostic sign** of corneal ulcer is',
    options:['Ciliary injection','Blepharospasm','Miosis','Positive fluorescein test'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**An ulcer is by definition a break in the epithelium, and fluorescein is the test that shows a break in the epithelium — so a positive stain is not merely suggestive, it is the diagnosis.** `L15,16) Cornea` supplies the logic in its layers slide: the epithelium has \"Tight junctions → barrier function\" and \"**Stains with fluorescein when disrupted**\". Nothing else in the option list is specific.\n\n**Read the four options as a hierarchy of specificity:**\n\n| Sign | What it tells you | Why it is not *sure* |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ciliary injection** | The deep, circumcorneal, violet-pink injection of anterior segment inflammation. The deck lists \"epithelial defect with stromal infiltrate **+ circumcorneal injection**\" among the signs of bacterial keratitis. | Present in **iridocyclitis and acute glaucoma too** — this bank makes exactly that point at `opqb-t8-309`. It localises the inflammation to the anterior segment; it does not say the epithelium is broken. |\n| **Blepharospasm** | Reflex lid squeezing, part of the irritative triad with lacrimation and photophobia. | A response to **pain from any surface disease** — a foreign body, an abrasion, a severe conjunctivitis. Non-specific to the point of uselessness for diagnosis. |\n| **Miosis** | A small pupil from **ciliary/sphincter spasm**, which is why this bank keys a cycloplegic as the universally indicated drug at `opqb-t8-307`. | A **secondary** consequence of the associated iritis, and it appears in iridocyclitis without any ulcer at all. |\n| **Positive fluorescein test** | The epithelium is missing, right here, this shape and this size. | **Nothing.** It is the definition made visible. |\n\n**Why the test is worth more than a diagnosis — it gives you the pattern too.** The *shape* of the green area is often the aetiology: **linear branching with terminal buds** = herpes simplex dendrite (`L15,16)`: \"terminal buds on branches = pathognomonic of HSV dendrite\"); **large irregular** = geographic; **round central with a dense infiltrate and hypopyon** = bacterial; **feathery with satellites** = fungal; **ring** = Acanthamoeba until proved otherwise. And the *size* decides admission: the deck admits for a \"central ulcer >2 mm\".\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The corollary matters as much as the answer: a **negative** stain in a painful red eye means the epithelium is intact, so whatever is wrong is deeper or elsewhere — iritis, scleritis, angle closure. Both readings of the test are informative, which is why it is the one investigation that belongs at every red-eye examination.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A positive fluorescein stain is the sure sign of a corneal ulcer because it demonstrates the epithelial defect itself; ciliary injection, blepharospasm and miosis all occur without any ulcer.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.46 (Q302), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-303', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The effective treatment of **dendritic ulcer** of the cornea is:',
    options:['Surface anesthesia','Local corticosteroids','Systemic corticosteroids','Acyclovir ointment'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**A dendritic ulcer is herpes simplex virus replicating in the corneal epithelium, so the treatment is an antiviral applied to the epithelium.** `L15,16) Cornea` works the question as a scenario and prints the answer: \"A 30-year-old presents with a 3-day history of mild eye pain, photophobia, and redness. Slit lamp with fluorescein shows a linear branching lesion with terminal buds in the central cornea. What should you do? — **Correct Answer: Topical antiviral (aciclovir 3% ointment 5x/day or ganciclovir gel) – NO TOPICAL STEROIDS**.\" Its summary table repeats it: \"HSV epithelial: **topical aciclovir 3%**\".\n\n**Two adjuncts the deck adds, and one it forbids:**\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Debridement** | \"Debridement of infected epithelium (swab) can be **adjunctive**\" — mechanically removing virus-laden cells |\n| **Oral prophylaxis** | \"Prophylactic oral aciclovir **400 mg twice daily** reduces recurrence rate by **~50%**\" — for recurrent disease, not for the acute dendrite |\n| **Steroids** | \"**Do NOT use steroids at this stage → risk of geographic ulcer → perforation**\" |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why |\n|---|---|\n| **Surface anesthesia** | Not a treatment at all — a diagnostic aid, and one the deck bans from therapeutic use in its strongest terms: \"**NEVER prescribe topical anaesthetics for outpatient analgesia**\", because they \"Inhibit epithelial healing · Cause stromal melting · Anaesthetic keratopathy → perforation\". Giving it here would produce the exact catastrophe the steroid options also produce. This bank keys the same point at `opqb-t8-348`. |\n| **Local corticosteroids** | The single most dangerous answer on the list — see `opqb-t8-301`. Steroid onto an epithelial dendrite promotes viral replication, enlarges it into a geographic ulcer and risks perforation. |\n| **Systemic corticosteroids** | The same immunosuppression by another route, with none of the antiviral cover that makes steroid safe in the **disciform** stage. |\n\n**Recognising the lesion**, because the treatment hangs on it: a **linear branching** epithelial ulcer with **terminal buds** at the ends of the branches, staining green with fluorescein at its bed and with rose bengal at its margins — the deck calls the terminal buds \"pathognomonic of HSV dendrite\", and distinguishes the herpes **zoster** pseudodendrite as \"smaller, stellate, **lacks terminal buds**\".\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Antiviral, no steroid, and warn the patient it will recur — this bank asks that recurrence twice more, at `opqb-t8-312` and `opqb-t8-344`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Dendritic (epithelial herpes simplex) keratitis is treated with a topical antiviral — aciclovir 3 % ointment — never with steroids and never with topical anaesthetic.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.46 (Q303), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-304', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Herpes simplex keratitis** is characterized by:',
    options:['Presence of pus in the anterior chamber','No tendency to recurrence','Corneal hypoesthesia','Tendency to Perforate'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Herpes damages the corneal nerves, and a cornea with damaged nerves is a numb cornea — that is the feature on this list that belongs to herpes and to nothing else here.** `L15,16) Cornea` lists the causes of neurotrophic keratopathy as \"**HSV**, HZV, acoustic neuroma surgery, DM, topical anaesthetic abuse\", and defines it as \"trigeminal anaesthesia → epithelial breakdown → persistent epithelial defect → melting\". The same bank prints a box making the link explicit at `opqb-t8-298`: \"HSV can cause neurotrophic keratitis → decreased corneal sensation due to trigeminal nerve involvement.\"\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and two of them are wrong because they belong to a different organism:**\n\n| Option | Which disease it actually describes |\n|---|---|\n| **Presence of pus in the anterior chamber** | **Hypopyon** — a hallmark of **bacterial** keratitis (\"Bacterial: contact lens / trauma → purulent ulcer + hypopyon\") and of **fungal** keratitis (\"feathery/satellite lesions, dry rough surface, **hypopyon**\"). This bank keys pneumococcus as the commonest cause of a hypopyon ulcer at `opqb-t8-306`. Herpes simplex epithelial disease does not do this. |\n| **No tendency to recurrence** | **The exact opposite of the truth**, and the reason the deck prescribes prophylaxis at all: \"Recurrences: consider prophylactic oral aciclovir 400 mg BD\", which \"reduces recurrence rate by ~50%\". This bank keys recurrence as the true statement at `opqb-t8-344` and again at `opqb-t8-312`. |\n| **Tendency to Perforate** | Perforation is not a feature of the ordinary dendritic ulcer — it is what happens when the disease is **mistreated with steroid** (`opqb-t8-301`) or when it becomes **necrotizing stromal keratitis**, which the deck describes separately: \"Progressive stromal necrosis & thinning · May lead to perforation · Very difficult to treat\". A complication of a subtype, not a characteristic of the disease. |\n\n**⚠️ Sibling question, different key.** `opqb-t8-344` prints the same lead-in — \"Herpes simplex keratitis is characterized by the following\" — with a different option set, and there the keyed answer is **recurrence**. Both are true of the disease; which one is keyed depends on which is offered. Read the options, not the stem.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Test corneal sensation **before** instilling anything, in any red eye you suspect of herpes. A reduced blink and a numb cornea explain why these patients under-report, heal slowly and keep coming back — and they are the reason the deck's neurotrophic treatments are protective rather than curative.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Herpes simplex keratitis reduces corneal sensation (V1 damage → neurotrophic keratopathy); hypopyon belongs to bacterial and fungal ulcers, and recurrence is the rule, not the exception.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.46 (Q304), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-305', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'**Advanced keratoconus** is **least** to be corrected when treated by:',
    options:['Hard contact Lens,','Rigid gas permeable (RGP) contact lens','Spectacles.','Keratoplasty'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Keratoconus makes the cornea irregular, and spectacles cannot correct irregular astigmatism — a spectacle lens has one regular curve and the cornea in front of it no longer has one.** `L15,16) Cornea` states the problem class first: the ectasias \"All cause **irregular astigmatism → reduced best spectacle-corrected visual acuity**\". A rigid lens works precisely because it does not try to correct the cornea; it **replaces its front surface** with a smooth one, and the tear film fills the gap between.\n\n**The deck's own management ladder, in its printed order:**\n\n| Rung | Treatment | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Spectacles** | \"early, mild\" — the deck says so in as many words |\n| 2 | Soft / **rigid** contact lenses | as irregularity develops |\n| 3 | **Scleral lenses** | \"advanced astigmatism\" |\n| 4 | **Corneal collagen cross-linking (CXL)** | to \"stabilize progression with UV-A + riboflavin\" |\n| 5 | **Intracorneal ring segments (ICRS / Intacs)** | selected cases |\n| 6 | **Keratoplasty: DALK (preferred) or PKP** | when the rest fail |\n\nSpectacles sit on rung 1, explicitly labelled *early, mild*. The stem says **advanced**, which puts it at the far end of that ladder — so spectacles are the option that will help least.\n\n**Why the other three do work in advanced disease:**\n\n| Option | Why it corrects |\n|---|---|\n| **Hard contact Lens** | A rigid front surface vaults the cone and neutralises the irregularity through the tear lens. |\n| **Rigid gas permeable (RGP) contact lens** | The same optics with oxygen transmission — the deck's rung 2, and the lens the patient in `opqb-t8-340` is already wearing successfully at 6/9. |\n| **Keratoplasty** | Rung 6: replaces the ectatic cornea outright. **DALK preferred** because it \"Preserves host endothelium… No endothelial rejection risk; lower overall rejection\", with PKP for scarring, perforation or failed DALK. |\n\n**⚠️ Its sibling has a different key.** `opqb-t8-335` asks the near-identical question — advanced keratoconus, what can it **NOT** be managed by — but offers *soft* contact lenses instead of spectacles, and keys **soft lenses**. Both keys follow the same principle: whatever is too **flexible** to hold a regular front surface fails. A soft lens drapes over the cone and reproduces its shape; a spectacle lens sits too far away to help at all. Read the option list before answering either.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The tell that a refraction is failing because of ectasia rather than because of a wrong prescription is the one this bank paints at `opqb-t8-334`: glasses that never feel right, an acuity that will not correct, and a refraction the optometrist cannot pin down. That patient needs a **topography**, not another pair of glasses.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Spectacles correct only regular astigmatism, so they are useless in advanced keratoconus; rigid lenses replace the irregular front surface and keratoplasty replaces the cornea.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.46 (Q305), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-306', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The commonest cause of **hypopyon corneal ulcer** is:',
    options:['Moraxella','Gonococcus','Pneumococcus','Staphylococcus'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The classical hypopyon ulcer — *ulcus serpens* — is pneumococcal.** *Streptococcus pneumoniae* produces a central, greyish, spreading ulcer with an undermined advancing edge and a sterile hypopyon in the anterior chamber; the pus is an exudative reaction to toxin diffusing across an intact Descemet's membrane, not infected pus, which is why draining it is unnecessary and treating the cornea clears it.\n\n**What the material supplies.** `L15,16) Cornea` names *S. pneumoniae* among the Gram-positive causes of bacterial keratitis, and lists hypopyon twice — \"Moderate–severe: stromal oedema, **hypopyon**, posterior synechiae, chemosis\" and, in the red-eye differential, \"Bacterial Keratitis: … Large central/paracentral ulcer · **Hypopyon common**\". It does **not** rank the organisms by how often each produces a hypopyon; measured, not assumed — *serpens*, *serpiginous* and *hypopyon ulcer* return no hits in any cached deck or book chapter, and the classical name and description below are therefore **not taken from the course material**.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What the material gives it |\n|---|---|\n| **Moraxella** | \"**Moraxella**: elderly, debilitated patients\" (`L15,16)`). A real corneal pathogen of the depleted host — classically an indolent inferior ulcer — but not the commonest hypopyon ulcer. |\n| **Gonococcus** | The organism the deck credits with penetrating **intact** epithelium, and the cause of hyperacute purulent conjunctivitis that can melt a cornea within days. Devastating, but uncommon; and the pus in gonococcal disease is conjunctival discharge, which is what makes it a tempting wrong answer. |\n| **Staphylococcus** | \"*Staphylococcus aureus*\" heads the Gram-positive list and is a very common keratitis organism overall — but it typically gives a **well-defined, localised** ulcer rather than the spreading hypopyon ulcer. It is also the antigen behind marginal keratitis. The strongest distractor by frequency alone. |\n\n**One organism the option list omits, and it is the one that will kill the eye.** *Pseudomonas aeruginosa* — the contact-lens organism — is described by the deck as \"rapid, aggressive\" and able to cause \"rapid descemetocele → **perforation within 24–48 h**\", with hypopyon and a greenish mucopurulent discharge. It is not offered here; do not let its absence make you forget it.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A hypopyon in a keratitis does not change the first move: the deck's CLINICAL PEARL is absolute — \"**Corneal scraping should be performed in most cases BEFORE starting antibiotics**\" — then intensive topical fluoroquinolone hourly, a cycloplegic for the ciliary spasm, and **no steroid** until the infection is controlled.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Pneumococcus is the classical cause of the hypopyon (serpiginous) corneal ulcer; the hypopyon is a sterile exudate, and the first action is still to scrape before antibiotics.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.46 (Q306), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-307', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'Irrespective of the etiology of a corneal ulcer, the drug **always** indicated is:',
    options:['Corticosteroid','Atropine','Antibiotics','Antifungal'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Cycloplegics** in Tx of corneal ulcer (to **relieve pain and discomfort caused by ciliary spasm**)\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Every corneal ulcer, whatever grew it, irritates the ciliary body — and a cycloplegic is the drug that answers that, so it is the one prescription that does not depend on the organism.** `L15,16) Cornea` lists it in the bacterial keratitis regimen — \"**Cycloplegic drops: reduce pain from ciliary spasm, prevent synechiae**\" — and again in its contact-lens scenario: \"Add cycloplegic (**cyclopentolate 1%**) for pain and synechiae prevention\".\n\n**Two jobs, and both are independent of aetiology:**\n\n| Job | How it works |\n|---|---|\n| **Analgesia** | Paralysing the ciliary muscle stops the spasm that produces the deep, aching, photophobic pain. This is why the pain of keratitis is not opioid-responsive but is cycloplegic-responsive. |\n| **Preventing posterior synechiae** | Dilating the pupil keeps the iris from adhering to the anterior lens capsule while the associated iritis is active — the deck lists \"posterior synechiae\" among the signs of moderate–severe bacterial keratitis. |\n\nA cycloplegic is also why **pilocarpine is banned** in an ulcer — it does the exact opposite. This bank keys that at `opqb-t8-315`, and its box there is the mirror image of this one: \"Pilocarpine: cholinergic miotic… **Causes ciliary spasm, increases pain**\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — read the word *irrespective*:**\n\n| Option | Why it is not universal |\n|---|---|\n| **Corticosteroid** | Not merely unnecessary but **contraindicated** in the two commonest aetiologies until the infection is controlled — bacterial (`opqb-t8-326`) and viral (`opqb-t8-301`). The deck: \"AVOID topical steroids until infectious cause is excluded or controlled.\" |\n| **Antibiotics** | The most tempting answer, and defensible in practice as broad cover while cultures are pending — but it is **aetiology-specific**: it does nothing for a fungal ulcer (which needs natamycin 5 % or amphotericin B 0.15 %), nothing for a dendritic ulcer (aciclovir), and nothing for Acanthamoeba (PHMB + propamidine). The stem's word is *irrespective*. |\n| **Antifungal** | Narrower still — reserved for filamentous fungi and Candida, and useless in every other ulcer. |\n\n*(Atropine specifically, as opposed to the class, is **not named in the course material** — the deck's named cycloplegic is cyclopentolate 1 %. Atropine is the longest-acting member of the same class and is the standard choice where a long block is wanted. The class fact — cycloplegic in every ulcer — is printed.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The prescription for an ulcer has three parts and only one of them is negotiable: the **anti-infective** chosen by the organism, the **cycloplegic** given to everyone, and the **steroid** withheld from everyone until the ophthalmologist says otherwise.",
    objective:'A cycloplegic is the one drug indicated in every corneal ulcer whatever the cause — it relieves ciliary-spasm pain and prevents posterior synechiae; the anti-infective depends on the organism.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.46 (Q307), key p.52 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-308', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Cornea** is supplied by nerve fibers derived from:',
    options:['Trochlear nerve','Optic nerve','Trigeminal nerve','Oculomotor nerve'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The cornea's sensory supply is the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve, and it is the most densely innervated tissue in the body.** `L15,16) Cornea` prints both facts on its opening anatomy slide: \"Most densely innervated tissue in the body (**trigeminal nerve, ophthalmic division**)\" and, in the Key Numbers panel, \"**Innervation: CN V1 (ophthalmic)**\".\n\nThe fibres reach the cornea as the **long ciliary nerves** from the nasociliary branch of V1, run radially into the stroma, lose their myelin as they enter — which is itself a condition of corneal transparency, keyed at `opqb-t8-311` and `opqb-t8-331` — and end as free nerve endings in the epithelium. *(The route by named branch is **not taken from the course material**; the deck names only V1. `opqb-t8-329` offers *nasocilliary* as a distractor, so the branch is worth knowing.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually supplies |\n|---|---|\n| **Trochlear nerve** (IV) | **Motor only** — the superior oblique muscle, and nothing else in the body. It carries no sensation at all. |\n| **Optic nerve** (II) | **Special sensory** — vision from the retina. It is a central tract, not a peripheral nerve, and it innervates no tissue in the sense meant here. Tempting only because both belong to sight. |\n| **Oculomotor nerve** (III) | **Motor and parasympathetic** — four extraocular muscles, levator palpebrae, and via the ciliary ganglion the sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle. It supplies the **pupil's constrictor**, which is why it appears as the wrong answer to the *efferent* question at `opqb-t8-329` too. |\n\n**Why this single fact carries so much of the topic:**\n\n| Consequence | Where it shows up |\n|---|---|\n| A cornea denervated by **HSV, HZV**, diabetes, acoustic neuroma surgery or anaesthetic abuse breaks down painlessly | Neurotrophic keratopathy — `opqb-t8-298`, `opqb-t8-304` |\n| **Herpes zoster ophthalmicus** follows the V1 dermatome, and the nasociliary branch supplies both the nose tip and the eye | **Hutchinson's sign** — `opqb-t8-343` |\n| The **afferent** limb of the blink reflex is V1; the efferent is VII | `opqb-t8-329` |\n| Corneal nerves cut by a LASIK flap cause post-operative dry eye | The deck credits SMILE with \"**Less corneal nerve damage → less dry eye**\" |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** V1 explains why the cornea is agonising when it is damaged and why it is silently destroyed when it is not — and the cotton-wisp test that distinguishes those two states costs nothing.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The cornea is supplied by CN V1 (ophthalmic division of trigeminal) via the long ciliary nerves — the most densely innervated tissue in the body, and the reason herpes denervates it.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.47 (Q308), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-309', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Ciliary injection** is **NOT** seen in:',
    options:['Herpetic keratitis','Bacterial ulcer','Chronic iridocyclitis','Catarrhal conjunctivitis','Acute iridocyclitis'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Ciliary injection is deep injection, and conjunctivitis is a superficial disease — so the one option on this list that is not an anterior-segment inflammation is the exception.** Four of the five entries inflame the cornea or the uvea, both of which share the deep **anterior ciliary** vessels that run to the limbus; catarrhal conjunctivitis inflames the **conjunctival** vessels, which are superficial, mobile and maximal in the fornices.\n\n**The two injections, side by side:**\n\n| | Conjunctival injection | **Ciliary (circumcorneal) injection** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Vessels | Superficial conjunctival | Deep anterior ciliary / episcleral |\n| Maximal at | The **fornices**, fading towards the limbus | The **limbus**, fading away from it |\n| Colour | Bright red | Deeper pink-violet |\n| Moves with the conjunctiva | **Yes** | **No** |\n| Blanches with topical vasoconstrictor | **Yes** | Poorly |\n| Means | Surface disease | **Cornea, iris or angle** — refer |\n\n**What the material supplies.** `L15,16) Cornea` lists \"epithelial defect with stromal infiltrate + **circumcorneal injection**\" among the signs of bacterial keratitis — the printed anchor for option B. It does not tabulate the two injection patterns; measured, not assumed, *ciliary injection* as a phrase appears in the cornea deck only in that compound. *(The comparison table above is therefore **not taken from the course material**; it is standard, uncontested red-eye examination.)* This bank builds two further questions on the same distinction — `opqb-t8-302`, where ciliary injection is rejected as a *sure* sign of ulcer precisely because it is shared, and the topic-7 phenylephrine table at `opqb-t7-291`, which is the same superficial-versus-deep idea tested with a drop.\n\n**Why each of the other four does show it:**\n\n| Option | Why the deep vessels are involved |\n|---|---|\n| **Herpetic keratitis** | A corneal disease; the limbal vessels supplying the cornea are recruited. |\n| **Bacterial ulcer** | Printed in the deck, as above — and the more severe the ulcer, the more marked. |\n| **Chronic iridocyclitis** | Uveal inflammation shares the anterior ciliary supply. Chronic disease shows it less floridly than acute, which is why it is the second-best wrong answer here. |\n| **Acute iridocyclitis** | The textbook cause: intense circumcorneal flush with a small, sluggish, irregular pupil. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Look at *where the redness is worst*. Redness that dies away as it approaches the cornea is a surface problem and can usually be treated where the patient stands. Redness that is worst in a ring **at the limbus** means cornea, iris or angle — and that eye needs the slit lamp, the fluorescein and, often, the same-day referral.\n\n*(Five options, A–E, as printed.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Conjunctiva — catarrhal conjunctivitis is taught there, but the question tests the corneal/uveal injection pattern.)*",
    objective:'Ciliary (circumcorneal) injection means cornea, iris or angle; conjunctivitis produces superficial conjunctival injection maximal in the fornices and blanching with a vasoconstrictor.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.47 (Q309), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-310', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'A **30-year-old male** presents with a history of **injury to the eye with a leaf 5 days ago** and pain, photophobia and redness of the eye for **2 days**. What would be the most likely pathology?',
    options:['Anterior uveitis','Conjunctivitis','Fungal corneal ulcer','Corneal laceration'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Fungal corneal ulcer** Risk factors: **trauma with vegetative matter,** delayed onset (2–5 days), pain, redness, photophobia\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Two details in the stem are doing all the work: the injuring object was a LEAF, and the symptoms began THREE DAYS AFTER it.** `L15,16) Cornea` supplies both. Under bacterial keratitis risk factors it separates the epidemiology: \"Contact lens wear (**most common in developed world**) · Ocular trauma (**agricultural injury – developing world**)\", and adds the instruction \"In tropics: **Fungal must be excluded!**\". Under fungal keratitis it names the organisms by the injury: \"Filamentous fungi (**Aspergillus, Fusarium**): **trauma with plant matter**\", against \"**Candida**: immunocompromised, contact lens wearers, topical steroids\".\n\n**The delay is the second half of the diagnosis.** A bacterial ulcer after an injury declares itself within hours to a day; this eye was quiet for three days and then became painful, and the deck says why: \"**Diagnosis often delayed – mimics bacterial keratitis**\" and \"Fungal: plant trauma / tropics → feathery satellite lesions → **slower onset**\".\n\n**What you would expect to find:** \"Signs: **feathery/satellite lesions, dry rough surface, hypopyon**\" — a greyish-white infiltrate with indistinct feathery edges, small satellite islands around the main lesion, and a dry, elevated, rough surface, quite unlike the sharply demarcated, wet-looking bacterial ulcer.\n\n**Investigation and treatment**, both printed:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Culture | \"**Sabouraud agar** (may take weeks)\" — and the general rule still applies: scrape **before** any drop |\n| Filamentous | \"**Natamycin 5%** – first-line\" |\n| Candida | \"**Amphotericin B 0.15%** – first-line\" |\n| Alternatives | \"voriconazole 1–2%, econazole 1%\" |\n\nSteroids are contraindicated — the deck lists \"topical steroids history\" as a **risk factor** for fungal keratitis in its red-eye differential.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Anterior uveitis** | Traumatic iritis exists and gives pain and photophobia — but it follows **blunt** injury, appears within a day or two, and does not need a *leaf* in the history. It would also not produce a corneal lesion; the deck's uveitis link here is only as a complication of keratitis. |\n| **Conjunctivitis** | Would not cause the photophobia and pain described, and no organism in the deck's conjunctivitis list is acquired from foliage. |\n| **Corneal laceration** | A leaf can certainly abrade or lacerate — but a laceration is **immediate**: pain from the moment of injury, not three days of quiet. The interval is what excludes it, and it is the strongest distractor. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** *Vegetable matter + a delay = fungus until proved otherwise.* Ask what the eye was hit with. In an agricultural injury, culture on Sabouraud as well as blood agar, start natamycin, and do not reach for a steroid.",
    objective:'Trauma with plant matter followed by a 2–5 day delay before symptoms is the classical fungal corneal ulcer (filamentous — Aspergillus, Fusarium); look for feathery satellite lesions and culture on Sabouraud.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.47 (Q310), key p.52 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-311', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Corneal transparency** is due to all of the following factors **EXCEPT**',
    options:['Compact arrangement of corneal stromal fibers','Lack of blood vessels','Myelinated nerve fibers','Non - keratinized stratified squamous epithelium'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Corneal nerves are UNmyelinated once they enter the cornea, and that is itself a condition of transparency — myelin scatters light.** `L15,16) Cornea` says the cornea is \"Most densely innervated tissue in the body\", and this bank's own five-option version of the same question, `opqb-t8-331`, prints the correct statement as an option: \"The cornea is avascular; **the nerve endings are unmyelinated**.\" Option C here states the opposite, so it is the exception.\n\n**The four real conditions of transparency**, three of them offered here as the true statements:\n\n| Factor | What the deck prints |\n|---|---|\n| **Regular stromal architecture** | \"**Orthogonally arranged collagen lamellae**\" making up 90 % of corneal thickness — uniform fibril diameter and spacing so that scattered light cancels by interference. `opqb-t8-331` adds \"fine collagenous fibers of the **same refractive index**\". |\n| **Avascularity** | \"**Completely avascular**; nutrients supplied by aqueous humour (posterior) and tears (anterior)\" — no vessels to absorb or scatter light. |\n| **Non-keratinised epithelium** | \"5–6 cell layers thick… Tight junctions → barrier function\" — smooth, unkeratinised stratified squamous surface. Keratin would be opaque. |\n| **Deturgescence** | \"**Endothelium is critical for maintaining transparency via active fluid pumping**\" — the Na/K ATPase of the single endothelial layer keeps the stroma relatively dehydrated. This is the factor **omitted** from this question's option list and supplied in `opqb-t8-331` as \"The cornea is deturgescent\". |\n\nUnmyelinated nerves belong on the same list as the four above, which is exactly why the bank can make a true statement out of it in one question and a false one in another.\n\n**Why the other three are genuine factors:** see the table — A is the stromal lamellar architecture, B is the avascularity, D is the unkeratinised epithelium, and each is printed in the deck's opening anatomy slides.\n\n*(The physics — that scattering cancels when fibril spacing is smaller than the wavelength of light, and that oedema disrupts it — is **not taken from the course material**; the deck states the anatomy and the pump, not the optics.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Every corneal disease that clouds the cornea does it by breaking one of these five. Endothelial failure (Fuchs', bullous keratopathy, an acute IOP rise) breaks **deturgescence** — hence `opqb-t8-342`. Scarring breaks **lamellar regularity**: \"Damage → scarring & opacity\". Neovascularisation breaks **avascularity**. Band keratopathy and dystrophy deposit material where none belongs. Read a cloudy cornea by asking which of the five has failed.\n\n*(⚠️ Sibling: `opqb-t8-331` asks the same content with **five** options and keys goblet cells. Different exception, same list.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Corneal transparency needs regular lamellar stroma, avascularity, non-keratinised epithelium, endothelial deturgescence and UNMYELINATED nerves — myelin would scatter light.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.47 (Q311), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-312', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The following about **dendritic ulcer** is true:',
    options:['It is always bilateral','Perforation is the most frequent complication.','It is usually recurrent.','It is usually associated with thick hypopyon.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Herpes simplex is a latent virus. It lives in the trigeminal ganglion between attacks and travels back down the nerve when the host is stressed, so recurrence is the defining behaviour of the disease.** `L15,16) Cornea` treats recurrence as the fact that shapes management: \"**Recurrent episodes**\" is listed as a distinguishing feature of HSV keratitis in its red-eye differential, and the deck prescribes against it — \"**Prophylactic oral aciclovir 400 mg twice daily reduces recurrence rate by ~50%**\", \"Recurrences: consider prophylactic oral aciclovir 400 mg BD\". This bank keys the same fact again at `opqb-t8-344`, and builds a whole vignette on it at `opqb-t8-338`, where \"These symptoms are **recurrent**\" plus a preceding cold is the entire clue.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why |\n|---|---|\n| **It is always bilateral** | Herpes simplex keratitis is characteristically **unilateral**. Bilateral disease is unusual and suggests atopy or immunosuppression. \"Always\" makes it doubly false. The bilateral disease the deck does teach is the **dystrophy** group — \"bilateral, primarily non-inflammatory, heritable\" — and `opqb-t8-347`-type exposure disease. |\n| **Perforation is the most frequent complication.** | Perforation is a **rare** outcome and, per the deck, largely an iatrogenic one: it follows steroid misuse (\"Can cause geographic ulcer · Risk of perforation\") or the uncommon **necrotizing stromal** form (\"Progressive stromal necrosis & thinning · May lead to perforation\"). The most frequent complication of ordinary dendritic disease is **recurrence with scarring**. |\n| **It is usually associated with thick hypopyon.** | Hypopyon belongs to **bacterial** and **fungal** ulcers (`opqb-t8-306`, `opqb-t8-310`). A dendritic ulcer is a thin epithelial lesion with little anterior chamber reaction. |\n\n**What recurrence changes in practice.** Each attack scars a little more stroma and denervates a little more cornea, so the patient accumulates both opacity and anaesthesia — which is why long-term prophylaxis is offered at all, why a red eye in a known herpes patient is assumed to be herpes until stained, and why **no steroid** may be given on spec (`opqb-t8-301`, `opqb-t8-303`).\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Ask every patient with a branching ulcer whether this has happened before. If it has, they need a conversation about prophylaxis, not just a tube of aciclovir.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Dendritic (herpes simplex) ulcer is typically unilateral and RECURRENT — the virus is latent in the trigeminal ganglion — and prophylactic oral aciclovir halves the recurrence rate.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.47 (Q312), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-313', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'All the following are signs of **keratoconus** **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Frequent vigorous eye rubbing is a risk factor','Munson sign','Kayser Fleisher ring','More in patients with atopy'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The Kayser-Fleischer ring is copper in Descemet's membrane and it belongs to Wilson's disease, not to keratoconus. The ring keratoconus has is the FLEISCHER ring — iron, at the base of the cone — and the two get confused because they share a surname.**\n\n`L15,16) Cornea` gives keratoconus its own diagnosis panel, and every other option on this list is in it:\n\n| Option | What the deck prints |\n|---|---|\n| **Frequent vigorous eye rubbing** | \"Risk factors: **eye rubbing**, atopy, Down syndrome, connective tissue disorders\", and the panel ends with the instruction \"**Avoid eye rubbing!**\" |\n| **Munson sign** | \"**Munson's sign: V-shaped deformity of lower lid on downgaze**\" — the cone indenting the lid margin, a sign of advanced disease |\n| **More in patients with atopy** | \"Risk factors: eye rubbing, **atopy**, Down syndrome, connective tissue disorders\" — and the two are linked, because atopic eyes are rubbed |\n\nThe rest of the deck's keratoconus signs, none of them offered here: \"Progressive bilateral (often asymmetric) corneal thinning and protrusion · Onset: **puberty**; progression until **3rd–4th decade** · **Vogt's striae**: vertical stromal stress lines (stretch marks) · **Acute hydrops**: sudden stromal oedema from Descemet's rupture · Topography: **inferior steepening, asymmetric bowtie pattern**\".\n\n**⚠️ Measured absence.** *Fleischer* and *Fleisher* return **zero hits across all 29 cached ophthalmology decks and all 12 cached book chapters** — the Fleischer ring is taught nowhere in this module, and neither is the Kayser-Fleischer ring; the latter exists here only as this printed distractor. *(Both rings, and the Wilson's-disease attribution, are therefore **not taken from the course material**. They are standard and uncontested.)* The distinction, for completeness: **Fleischer ring** = iron deposited in the **epithelium** in a ring at the **base of the cone**, best seen with a cobalt blue filter; **Kayser-Fleischer ring** = copper in **Descemet's membrane** at the **limbus**, seen on gonioscopy in Wilson's disease.\n\n**⚠️ A defect in the question, recorded and not corrected.** The stem asks for **signs**, but options A and D are **risk factors**, not signs — the deck itself files them under \"Risk factors\". On a strict reading three of the four options are not signs. The key is still C, and C is the only option that has nothing to do with keratoconus at all, which is what the question means to test.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A young patient with a rising astigmatism who rubs their eyes and has eczema or hay fever is being told the diagnosis before the slit lamp is switched on. Get a **topography** (`opqb-t8-341`) and tell them to stop rubbing.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Keratoconus gives Munson’s sign, Vogt’s striae, a Fleischer IRON ring, hydrops and inferior steepening on topography; the Kayser-Fleischer copper ring belongs to Wilson’s disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.47 (Q313), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-314', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'In **keratoconus**:',
    options:['Munson sign is present','Corneal topography is diagnostic','Corneal opacity may be seen','All of the above'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**All three statements are printed in the deck's keratoconus panel, so the answer is D.** Taking them one at a time:\n\n| Statement | Where `L15,16) Cornea` supports it |\n|---|---|\n| **Munson sign is present** | \"**Munson's sign: V-shaped deformity of lower lid on downgaze**\" — the protruding cone pushes the lid margin into a V as the patient looks down. A sign of **advanced** disease, which is why `opqb-t8-325` keys as false the claim that it \"occurs early\". |\n| **Corneal topography is diagnostic** | \"Topography: **inferior steepening, asymmetric bowtie pattern**\", and \"Screening for ectasia is **MANDATORY** before refractive surgery (corneal topography)\". It is also the **earliest** test — `opqb-t8-341`, whose box says topography \"maps entire corneal surface, sensitive to **very early, subclinical** irregularities\". |\n| **Corneal opacity may be seen** | Two routes in the deck. **Vogt's striae** — \"vertical stromal stress lines (stretch marks)\" — and, more substantially, **acute hydrops**: \"sudden stromal oedema from **Descemet's rupture**\", which resolves leaving a scar. That scar is what forces the graft in `opqb-t8-340`. |\n\n**Why *All of the above* is right rather than lazy.** Each of the first three describes a different stage, and together they are the natural history: topography detects it **before** anything is visible, opacity appears when the cornea has stretched or ruptured, and Munson's sign appears when the cone is gross enough to deform the lid. A candidate who rejects D is usually rejecting *Munson* on frequency grounds — but the option says \"is present\", not \"is present early\".\n\n**The rest of the picture, for the other keratoconus questions in this topic:** \"Progressive **bilateral** (often asymmetric) corneal thinning and protrusion · Onset: **puberty**; progression until **3rd–4th decade** · Risk factors: **eye rubbing, atopy, Down syndrome, connective tissue disorders**\".\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Topography is the instrument that changed this disease, in both directions: it finds keratoconus early enough for **cross-linking** to stop it, and it finds *forme fruste* disease in refractive-surgery candidates early enough to stop the surgeon (`opqb-t8-337`). The deck's red flag says it in five words: \"Keratoconus-like topography → **do NOT operate**\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Keratoconus can show Munson’s sign, corneal opacity from striae or resolved hydrops, and a diagnostic topography (inferior steepening, asymmetric bowtie) — all three are true.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.47 (Q314), key p.52' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-315', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'Treatment of **simple corneal ulcer** should **NOT** include:',
    options:['Antibiotic drops','Pilocarpine eye drops','Bandage','Antibiotic ointment','Atropine eye ointment'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Pilocarpine:** cholinergic miotic, contracts the pupil Contraindicated in corneal ulcer:\n**Causes ciliary spasm, increases pain**\n**Can worsen inflammation**\n**Bandage → sometimes used to protect the cornea and reduce pain**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Pilocarpine is the exact opposite of what a corneal ulcer needs.** An ulcer hurts because the ciliary muscle goes into spasm; the standard prescription is therefore a **cycloplegic**, which paralyses that muscle — `L15,16) Cornea`: \"**Cycloplegic drops: reduce pain from ciliary spasm, prevent synechiae**\", and \"Add cycloplegic (cyclopentolate 1%) for pain and synechiae prevention\". Pilocarpine is a **cholinergic agonist**: it contracts the ciliary muscle and the sphincter pupillae, so it deepens the spasm, worsens the pain, and by constricting the pupil it invites the iris to stick to the lens as posterior synechiae. This bank keys the mirror-image question at `opqb-t8-307`, where a cycloplegic is *always* indicated.\n\n**Why the other four are legitimate:**\n\n| Option | Why it belongs |\n|---|---|\n| **Antibiotic drops** | The mainstay: \"First-line: **intensive topical fluoroquinolones** (ciprofloxacin or ofloxacin) – hourly day 1, then taper\" — with the standing rule that scraping comes first. |\n| **Bandage** | The bank's own box: \"sometimes used to **protect the cornea and reduce pain**\". A bandage contact lens shields the exposed nerve endings from the lid. *(⚠️ Note the tension with `opqb-t8-328`, where **patching** is keyed as contraindicated in bacterial keratitis: a **bandage lens** on a clean epithelial defect is not the same as an **occlusive pad** over an infected one. The stem here says *simple*.)* |\n| **Antibiotic ointment** | Same drug class, longer contact time — the usual overnight formulation. |\n| **Atropine eye ointment** | The cycloplegic itself, in ointment form. Keyed as the universally indicated drug at `opqb-t8-307`. |\n\n**Why *simple* matters in the stem.** The bank's *simple corneal ulcer* means an uncomplicated one: an epithelial defect without deep infiltration, hypopyon or impending perforation. The regimen is therefore antibiotic + cycloplegic + protection, with **no steroid** in any circumstance until the infection is controlled (`opqb-t8-301`, `opqb-t8-326`) and **no topical anaesthetic** ever (`opqb-t8-348`).\n\n*(Five options, A–E, as printed. Pilocarpine's pharmacology beyond the bank's box — that it is the miotic used to open the angle in acute angle closure, which is why it is a familiar drop at all — is **not taken from `L15,16) Cornea`**; it is taught in the glaucoma decks.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Dilate an ulcer, never constrict it. If the pupil in front of you is small and the eye is painful, the drug you want is the one that makes it big.",
    objective:'Pilocarpine is contraindicated in corneal ulcer — it is a miotic that worsens ciliary spasm and pain; ulcers get antibiotic, a cycloplegic such as atropine, and protection.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.47 (Q315), key p.53 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-316', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'**Pre Lasik** we do:',
    options:['Topography & pachymetry','Biometry','Biometry & topography','Keratometry'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Two measurements decide whether a cornea may be lasered: its SHAPE and its THICKNESS. Topography gives the shape, pachymetry gives the thickness, and each rules out a different disaster.** `L15,16) Cornea` prints them as the first two items of its *Pre-op Must-Checks* panel:\n\n> **Corneal topography** – RULE OUT ectasia\n> **Pachymetry** – minimum thickness\n> Stable refraction ≥1 year\n> Adequate tear film (**Schirmer's, TBUT**)\n> Pupil size in dim light\n> Discontinue CLs before mapping\n\nand repeats them in prose: \"Mandatory pre-op assessment: **corneal topography, pachymetry**, pupil size, tear film, refraction stability (≥1 yr)\".\n\n**The numbers that make them decisive**, from the deck's ectasia-screening slide:\n\n| Check | Threshold |\n|---|---|\n| Central corneal thickness | **≥500 µm**; \"<500 µm is a **relative** contraindication\" |\n| Residual stromal bed after ablation | **≥300 µm**; \"RSB <300 µm = **absolute** contraindication for LASIK\" |\n| Topography pattern | \"asymmetric bowtie, inferior steepening = **contraindication**\" |\n| Refraction stability | ≥1 year |\n| Age | \"≥21 years\" in the checklist; \"typically ≥18–21 years\" in the prose |\n\n**Why it matters this much:** \"**Ectasia is the most feared long-term complication of LASIK**\", and its risk factors are \"forme fruste keratoconus, thin cornea, young age, high myopia, low residual stromal bed\". The deck's red flag is blunt — \"Keratoconus-like topography → **do NOT operate** (LASIK)\" — and its top-ten list includes \"Keratoconus screening is **mandatory** before refractive surgery\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually measures |\n|---|---|\n| **Biometry** | **Axial length** and keratometry for **intraocular lens power calculation** — a *cataract* investigation, not a refractive-surgery one. It says nothing about corneal thickness or regularity. |\n| **Biometry & topography** | Half right and half irrelevant: topography belongs, biometry does not, and pachymetry — the check that prevents ectasia — is missing. The best distractor. |\n| **Keratometry** | Measures the **central** corneal curvature only (`opqb-t8-317`), which the bank's own box at `opqb-t8-341` says \"detects advanced or moderate astigmatism, but **not early changes**\". A normal keratometry does not exclude forme fruste keratoconus, so it cannot be the pre-LASIK screen. |\n\n**⚠️ A scope note.** *Schirmer's* is named in this deck's tear-film check — it is **not** absent from the module, whatever `L9) lacrimal system.`'s own not-covered list says. A deck's absence list is scoped to that deck.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Topography rules out the shape you must not laser; pachymetry rules out the cornea you cannot afford to thin. Everything else on the list makes the outcome better; those two stop it being a catastrophe.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Pre-LASIK screening is corneal TOPOGRAPHY (rule out ectasia/forme fruste keratoconus) plus PACHYMETRY (≥500 µm central, residual stromal bed ≥300 µm); biometry is a cataract test.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.47 (Q316), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-317', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'**Keratometry** measures:',
    options:['Corneal curvature','Corneal epithelium integrity','Ciliary injection','Conjunctival injection'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Keratometry measures the curvature of the central cornea, and it reports it as a refractive power in dioptres along two principal meridians.** The bank prints the definition itself in its box at `opqb-t8-341`: \"**Keratometry – measures central corneal curvature**; detects advanced or moderate astigmatism, but not early changes.\"\n\n**What the two readings mean.** The instrument returns a steep and a flat meridian; the difference between them is the **corneal astigmatism**. `opqb-t2-77` is that arithmetic made into a question — 46 D vertical against 45 D horizontal is 1 D of corneal astigmatism — and its answer turns on the fact that keratometry measures **only the cornea**, so a patient who is emmetropic on retinoscopy despite corneal astigmatism must be compensating in the lens.\n\n**What it cannot do**, which is the other half of its examinable content: keratometry samples only a small **central** zone, typically a ring of about 3 mm, so it misses the **inferior** steepening where keratoconus begins. That is why the deck makes **topography** — \"maps entire corneal surface\" — the mandatory pre-refractive-surgery screen (`opqb-t8-316`) and the earliest indicator of keratoconus (`opqb-t8-341`), and why keratometry is a distractor in both.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What actually measures / shows it |\n|---|---|\n| **Corneal epithelium integrity** | **Fluorescein staining** — `opqb-t8-299`, `opqb-t8-302`. The deck: epithelium \"Stains with fluorescein when disrupted\". A keratometer reads a reflected mire off the tear film and says nothing about whether the epithelium beneath it is intact. |\n| **Ciliary injection** | A **clinical sign**, observed at the slit lamp (`opqb-t8-309`). Not a measurement at all. |\n| **Conjunctival injection** | Likewise an observed sign, and the superficial counterpart of the previous option. |\n\n**Where keratometry is genuinely used:** contact lens fitting (the base curve must match the cornea), **intraocular lens power calculation** in cataract surgery, where it is one half of biometry, and tracking astigmatism over time. *(These applications are **not taken from `L15,16) Cornea`**, which mentions keratometry only through this bank's shared box; they are standard and are taught in the cataract and refraction decks.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Keratometry answers *how steep is the middle of this cornea*. If the question is *is this cornea the wrong shape*, the instrument you need is a topographer.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Keratometry measures central corneal curvature in dioptres along two meridians — the corneal astigmatism — but samples only a small central zone and misses early keratoconus.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.47 (Q317), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-318', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The following statement is true about the **cornea**:',
    options:['It constitutes one fourth of the outer coat of the eyeball','Its average thickness at the center is 0.5 cm','The anterior corneal curvature is greater than the scleral curvature','The endothelium can regenerate within 7 days when damaged'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The cornea is more steeply curved than the sclera — that is why it bulges forward out of the scleral sphere, and why the junction between them, the limbus, is a visible groove.** Radius of corneal curvature is about 7.8 mm against about 11.5 mm for the sclera; the smaller the radius, the greater the curvature. That geometry is what makes the cornea the eye's dominant refracting surface, which the deck does print: \"**Largest refractive surface of the eye – provides ~75% of total ocular power**\", \"Refractive power: **~43 D**\". *(The two radii themselves are **not taken from the course material**; the deck gives corneal power and dimensions but no radius, and no cached source gives a scleral radius. The comparison is standard and uncontested.)*\n\n**Why each of the other three is false, and each is false against a printed number:**\n\n| Option | What the material actually says |\n|---|---|\n| **One fourth of the outer coat** | Book ch.7 p.97 prints the split on its anatomy figure: **sclera = 5/6** of the outer fibrous coat, **cornea = 1/6**. One sixth, not one quarter. |\n| **Average central thickness 0.5 cm** | `L15,16) Cornea`: \"**Central thickness: 540 µm**\", \"~540 µm thick centrally, ~700 µm peripherally\". 540 µm is **0.54 mm** — the option is out by a factor of about ten, since 0.5 **cm** would be 5 mm, thicker than the whole anterior chamber. A units trap: 0.5 **mm** would have been very nearly right. |\n| **Endothelium regenerates within 7 days** | The deck says the opposite, twice: \"Endothelium: single cell layer, Na/K ATPase pump · **Cannot regenerate**; cell density critical\". The seven days belongs to the **epithelium** — \"**Regenerates completely in 5–7 days**\" — so the option is a true fact attached to the wrong layer. |\n\n**The regeneration table the last option is testing**, worth holding whole because three questions in this topic touch it:\n\n| Layer | Regenerates? |\n|---|---|\n| **Epithelium** (50 µm) | **Yes — completely, in 5–7 days** |\n| **Bowman's layer** (12 µm) | **No** — \"Acellular, condensed collagen · Does NOT regenerate if damaged\" |\n| **Stroma** (90 % of thickness) | Heals by **scarring** — \"Damage → scarring & opacity\" |\n| **Descemet's** | Regenerated by the endothelium, being its basement membrane |\n| **Endothelium** | **No** — cells spread to cover losses; density falls permanently. \"Critical density: **<700 cells/mm²** → oedema risk\", from a normal \"**2,500–3,000 cells/mm²**\" |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The endothelial line is the one with consequences: every intraocular operation costs endothelial cells that are never replaced, which is why cell counts are monitored in Fuchs' dystrophy and why **DMEK/DSAEK** exist — to replace that one irreplaceable layer without disturbing the rest.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The cornea is more curved than the sclera, is 1/6 of the outer coat, is ~540 µm thick centrally, and its endothelium never regenerates — only the epithelium does, in 5–7 days.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.48 (Q318), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-319', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'The cornea receives laser in all of the following procedures **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['PRK','LASIK','PRP','PTK'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**PRK** (Photorefractive keratectomy): Excimer laser reshapes corneal stroma\n**LASIK** (Laser-assisted in situ keratomileusis): Laser ablates corneal stroma under flap\n**PRP** (Panretinal photocoagulation): **Laser applied to retina, not cornea**\n**PTK** (Phototherapeutic keratectomy): Laser removes superficial corneal opacities\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Three of the four acronyms end in K for *keratectomy* or *keratomileusis* — cornea. The odd one out is P for *photocoagulation*, and it is aimed at the retina.** That is the whole question, and the mnemonic is reliable.\n\n**What the deck adds to each corneal procedure:**\n\n| Procedure | `L15,16) Cornea` |\n|---|---|\n| **PRK** (surface ablation) | \"**Epithelium removed; ablation directly on Bowman's** · No flap → safer for contact sports / thin corneas · Slower recovery (3–5 days pain; weeks for clarity) · Risk: **subepithelial haze** (MMC applied to prevent) · Suitable when LASIK is contraindicated\" |\n| **LASIK** | \"**Flap created** (microkeratome / femtosecond) · Excimer laser ablates exposed stroma · Fast recovery (1–2 days) · Risk: flap dislocation, **ectasia**, DLK ('sands of Sahara') · Wide range: myopia up to **−10 D**\" |\n| **PTK** | \"Laser removes superficial corneal opacities\" — therapeutic rather than refractive; the deck also gives it as an alternative treatment for **band keratopathy** (`opqb-t8-297`) |\n\nTwo further corneal laser procedures the deck teaches but this question does not offer: **FemtoLASIK** (\"Femtosecond laser creates more precise, thinner flap… no blade used\") and **SMILE** (\"Femtosecond laser cuts lenticule inside intact cornea · Removed via 4 mm incision → **no flap** · **Less corneal nerve damage → less dry eye**\").\n\n**What PRP actually is.** Panretinal photocoagulation burns the ischaemic peripheral **retina** to make it stop producing VEGF, and so to regress neovascularisation. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` prints it as \"Laser panretinal photocoagulation\" and `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` as \"Treat neovascularization by panretinal\" photocoagulation. The laser passes **through** the cornea on its way in, but the cornea is not its target — which is exactly the distinction the option is testing.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Sort ophthalmic lasers by target, not by name: **cornea** — PRK, LASIK, PTK, SMILE; **retina** — PRP, focal/grid; **trabecular meshwork** — argon and selective laser trabeculoplasty; **posterior capsule and iris** — Nd:YAG. This bank tests the first and last of those groups together at `opqb-t8-323`.",
    objective:'PRK, LASIK and PTK all put laser on the cornea; PRP (panretinal photocoagulation) is aimed at the retina and only passes through the cornea.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.48 (Q319), key p.53 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-320', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The following conditions do **NOT** cause **exposure keratitis**:',
    options:['Facial nerve palsy','Proptosis','Grave’s disease','Levator muscle palsy'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Exposure keratitis needs the eye to be left uncovered, and a levator palsy does the opposite — it drops the upper lid over the cornea and protects it.** `L15,16) Cornea` defines the mechanism and names the causes in four lines: \"**Incomplete lid closure → inferior epithelial breakdown** · Causes: **VII nerve palsy (Bell's), thyroid eye disease, proptosis** · Treatment: lubricants, taping eyelid, moisture chamber, tarsorrhaphy.\"\n\nThree of this question's four options are that printed list verbatim; the fourth is not on it.\n\n**Why each of the other three exposes the cornea:**\n\n| Option | Mechanism |\n|---|---|\n| **Facial nerve palsy** | CN VII drives **orbicularis oculi**, the closing muscle. Paralyse it and the lids no longer meet — **lagophthalmos**. The deck names \"VII nerve palsy (Bell's)\" first. This is the same mechanism as `opqb-t8-347`, where reduced blink and incomplete closure in Parkinson's disease give inferior punctate erosions. |\n| **Proptosis** | The globe is pushed forward beyond the lids' reach, so they cannot cover it however hard the orbicularis works. |\n| **Grave’s disease** | Thyroid eye disease produces **both** — proptosis **and** upper lid retraction — so it is the double insult, and the deck names it as \"thyroid eye disease\". |\n\n**Why levator palsy is the exception.** The levator palpebrae superioris **opens** the eye; paralysing it gives **ptosis**, a lid that hangs low. A ptotic lid covers more cornea, not less. If anything a complete ptosis is protective — which is why a **tarsorrhaphy**, a surgically induced partial ptosis, is on the deck's own treatment list for exposure keratopathy.\n\n**Where the damage appears, and why that helps you recognise it:** **inferiorly**, in the strip of cornea the lid fails to cover — the deck says \"inferior epithelial breakdown\", and `opqb-t8-347`'s box says \"**Inferior** corneal punctate epithelial erosions, especially worse in the morning\". Worse in the morning because the eye has been drying all night.\n\n**The other route to the same picture**, which the deck teaches separately and which is worth not confusing with this one: **neurotrophic keratopathy** — the cornea is covered but **numb**, so it does not blink or water reflexly. Exposure = the lid fails. Neurotrophic = the nerve fails (`opqb-t8-298`).\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In any facial palsy, ask about the eye before anything else. Lubricate heavily, tape the lid at night, and consider a tarsorrhaphy early — a cornea can be lost to a Bell's palsy while everyone is watching the smile.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Orbit — thyroid eye disease and proptosis are taught in `op-orbit`, but the question tests the corneal consequence.)*",
    objective:'Exposure keratitis follows anything that stops the lids covering the cornea — VII palsy, proptosis, thyroid eye disease; levator palsy causes PTOSIS, which covers the cornea instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.48 (Q320), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-321', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The following are the clinical presentations of **herpes simplex keratitis** **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Superficial punctate keratitis','Dendritic keratitis','Central single ulcer with satellite lesions','Disciform keratitis'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Satellite lesions are the signature of FUNGAL keratitis, and the deck says so in the same breath every time it mentions them.** `L15,16) Cornea`, fungal keratitis: \"Signs: **feathery/satellite lesions**, dry rough surface, hypopyon\"; and in the red-eye differential, \"Fungal Keratitis: Trauma (plant matter) history · **Feathery satellite lesions** · Slower onset\". This bank keys the fungal diagnosis from exactly that picture at `opqb-t8-310`.\n\n**The three genuine forms of herpes simplex keratitis, as the deck classifies them:**\n\n| Form | What the deck prints |\n|---|---|\n| **Epithelial (dendritic / geographic)** | \"Active virus replication in epithelium · Classic **dendritic ulcer with terminal buds** · Fluorescein: stains the ulcer bed · Rose Bengal: stains virus-laden margins · **Geographic** = large irregular lesion\" |\n| **Disciform (stromal immune)** | \"**Immune-mediated endotheliitis** · Central corneal **disc-like oedema** · **Keratic precipitates** under area of oedema · Treat with **TOPICAL STEROIDS + antivirals**\" |\n| **Necrotizing stromal** | \"Combination of active virus + immune response · Progressive stromal necrosis & thinning · May lead to perforation · Very difficult to treat · Combined antiviral + steroid (careful balance)\" |\n\n**Superficial punctate keratitis** (option A) is the earliest epithelial expression — scattered fine staining dots before they coalesce into a dendrite. The deck names punctate epithelial keratitis explicitly for herpes **zoster** — \"Corneal manifestations: **punctate epithelial keratitis**, pseudodendritic ulcer, mucous plaques, disciform keratitis, neurotrophic keratopathy\" — and lists \"Epithelial keratitis\" among HSV's own forms. *(Punctate epithelial keratitis as the opening stage of HSV specifically is **not printed in so many words** in `L15,16)`; the deck gives it for HZO and gives HSV \"epithelial\" disease generally. Recorded so the option is not over-claimed. It remains true, and it is not the exception on this list.)*\n\n**Why the exception is unambiguous.** Nothing in the herpes group produces satellites: the herpetic lesion is **branching** (dendrite), **map-shaped** (geographic) or **disc-shaped** (disciform). A **central ulcer with separate satellite islands** around it is a fungus spreading into the stroma beyond the main lesion.\n\n**Why the distinction has consequences.** The two diseases are treated in opposite directions — herpes epithelial disease gets an **antiviral** and no steroid; fungal keratitis gets **natamycin 5 %** or **amphotericin B 0.15 %**, and steroids are listed by the deck as a **risk factor** for acquiring it. Mistaking one for the other and reaching for a steroid is the error the deck's whole *Common Prescribing Errors* table exists to prevent.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Read the shape of the stain. Branches with buds → herpes. Feathers with satellites → fungus. Ring → Acanthamoeba. Round and dense with hypopyon → bacteria.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Herpes simplex keratitis presents as punctate/dendritic/geographic epithelial disease, disciform stromal disease or necrotizing stromal disease; satellite lesions around a central ulcer mean FUNGUS.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.48 (Q321), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-322', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Band shaped keratopathy** is seen in the:',
    options:['Upper part of the cornea','Interpalpebral part of the cornea','Lower part of the cornea','Peripheral parts of the cornea'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The band sits in the strip of cornea the lids leave uncovered — the interpalpebral zone, running horizontally from 3 o'clock to 9 o'clock.** `L15,16) Cornea` prints the location with the substance and the layer in one line: \"**Band keratopathy: calcium deposits in Bowman's layer in interpalpebral zone**; associated with chronic ocular disease, hypercalcaemia, chronic uveitis.\" This bank asks the substance separately at `opqb-t8-297`, whose box adds the depth: \"fine dust-like calcium deposits in the sub-epithelium, Bowman's layer, and the anterior stroma\".\n\n**Why that strip and not another.** The exposed cornea is where the tear film evaporates fastest and where carbon dioxide escapes to the air; the local rise in surface pH shifts calcium out of solution and it precipitates in Bowman's layer. The band therefore starts as two small opacities at the limbus at 3 and 9 o'clock, spreads inwards, and eventually meets across the centre — with a **clear lucid interval at the limbus** and small round **holes** where nerves pierce Bowman's layer. *(The evaporation/pH mechanism and the honeycomb holes are **not taken from the course material** — the deck gives the zone, the layer and the causes, and no more. They are standard and uncontested.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What actually lives there |\n|---|---|\n| **Upper part of the cornea** | Superior lesions are the trachoma/vernal territory — superior pannus and limbal disease — because that is the strip the upper lid rubs. Not a deposition site. |\n| **Lower part of the cornea** | The **inferior** cornea is where **exposure** damage lands: \"inferior epithelial breakdown\" (`opqb-t8-320`) and \"Inferior corneal punctate epithelial erosions\" (`opqb-t8-347`). A different disease with a different mechanism. |\n| **Peripheral parts of the cornea** | The peripheral ring belongs to **arcus senilis** — \"lipid arc at limbus\" (`opqb-t8-346`), which characteristically has a **clear zone separating it from the limbus** — and to the peripheral ulcerative group: marginal keratitis, Mooren's ulcer, PUK. Band keratopathy is central-and-horizontal, not circumferential. The strongest distractor, because the band does begin peripherally. |\n\n**A note on the company it keeps.** The band is a marker of chronicity, so finding one should send you looking for its cause: the deck's list is \"Chronic anterior uveitis · **Hypercalcaemia** · Silicone oil in AC · End-stage ocular disease · Gout (hyperuricaemia) · Familial / hereditary · Idiopathic age-related\". In a child, chronic anterior uveitis means juvenile idiopathic arthritis until proved otherwise.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A horizontal grey band across the middle of the cornea with clear cornea above and below it, in an eye that has been inflamed for years — treat the eye if vision is affected (\"EDTA chelation… or excimer laser PTK\") and check the serum calcium in every case.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Band keratopathy occupies the INTERPALPEBRAL strip — the exposed 3-to-9 o’clock band — where calcium precipitates in Bowman’s layer.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.48 (Q322), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-323', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'**LASER** can be used to treat the following conditions **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Neovascular glaucoma.','Rubbing lashes.','Dendritic ulcer.','Cataract.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**\"Rubbing lashes\" is trichiasis, and the material's entire treatment list for trichiasis contains no laser.** Book ch.5, printed 78, gives four treatments and only four: \"**epilation, electrolysis, cryotherapy, mucous-membrane graft in the grey line**\". Measured, not assumed: *argon* returns exactly one hit across all cached ophthalmology decks and book chapters, and it is in `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` — \"Laser trabeculoplasty (Argon, Selective)\" — nothing to do with lashes. The only laser the eyelid chapter names anywhere is a **CO₂ laser for xanthelasma** (printed 80). *(Argon laser ablation of lash follicles does exist in practice as a fifth option for a few scattered lashes; it is **not taken from the course material**, and its absence from the material is what makes this key defensible.)*\n\n**Why the other three do receive laser:**\n\n| Option | The laser, and where the module teaches it |\n|---|---|\n| **Neovascular glaucoma** | **Panretinal photocoagulation** to the ischaemic retina, to make the new vessels regress: `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders`, \"Treat neovascularization by panretinal\" photocoagulation; `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy`, \"Laser panretinal photocoagulation\". Neovascular glaucoma is named as a secondary glaucoma in `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry`. Cyclodestructive laser is the other route in a blind painful eye. |\n| **Cataract.** | Two lasers, both real. **Nd:YAG capsulotomy** for the after-cataract that clouds the posterior capsule months after surgery — this bank keys \"YAG laser\" for capsulotomy in its very next topic, at Q349 on book p.54 — and **femtosecond laser-assisted cataract surgery** for the capsulorhexis and lens fragmentation. |\n| **Dendritic ulcer.** | ⚠️ **The soft option, recorded and not disputed.** No cached source gives a laser for a dendritic ulcer, and this same bank keys **acyclovir ointment** as its treatment at `opqb-t8-303`. What can be said for it: **PTK**, the excimer laser procedure the deck defines as removing \"superficial corneal opacities\", is used therapeutically on the anterior cornea, and mechanical **debridement** of infected epithelium is explicitly \"adjunctive\" in the deck. On a strict reading both B and C are laser-free in this material. **The bank keys B, and B is the better answer**, because trichiasis has a complete printed treatment list that excludes laser whereas the cornea is a routine laser target. Answer B. |\n\n**How to sort ophthalmic lasers by target**, which is what makes this class of question quick:\n\n| Target | Laser and procedure |\n|---|---|\n| **Retina** | Argon/diode photocoagulation — PRP, focal and grid |\n| **Trabecular meshwork** | Argon or selective laser trabeculoplasty |\n| **Iris** | Nd:YAG peripheral iridotomy |\n| **Posterior capsule** | Nd:YAG capsulotomy |\n| **Cornea** | Excimer — PRK, LASIK, PTK; femtosecond — flaps, SMILE |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** For an in-turning lash the answer is almost always mechanical: pull it, kill the follicle, or rebuild the lid margin — and treat the underlying blepharitis or cicatrisation, or it will grow back.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Lid — trichiasis is taught in `op-lid`, but the question tests laser applications.)*",
    objective:'Trichiasis is treated by epilation, electrolysis, cryotherapy or a grey-line graft — not laser; PRP treats neovascular glaucoma and Nd:YAG treats the after-cataract.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.48 (Q323), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-324', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'A **60-year-old female** patient presented with **vesicular eruptions of the right forehead; nose & scalp** associated with **neuralgic pain**:',
    options:['Typical corneal dendrites may occur.','The associated keratitis has no tendency to recur.','Topical acyclovir is sufficient to treat associated keratitis.','Paralytic strabismus is very unlikely to occur.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The stem is herpes zoster ophthalmicus: varicella-zoster reactivating in the V1 dermatome — forehead, scalp and nose, unilateral, with neuralgic pain.** `L15,16) Cornea`: \"Varicella-zoster virus reactivation in **CN V1 (ophthalmic) dermatome** · 1 in 3 people will develop herpes zoster in their lifetime · **Hutchinson's sign**: vesicles on tip/side of nose → **nasociliary branch involved** → HIGH risk of ocular complications.\" The nose is in this stem, so this patient is in the high-risk group — the point this bank keys directly at `opqb-t8-343`.\n\n**⚠️ A DECK-vs-BANK DIVERGENCE ON THE KEYED OPTION, RECORDED AND NOT CORRECTED.** The bank keys **A, \"Typical corneal dendrites may occur\"**. The deck is explicit that the zoster lesion is a **pseudo**dendrite and that it is *not* typical: \"Note: **HZO pseudodendrite is smaller, stellate, lacks terminal buds** (cf. HSV dendrite)\", and its corneal list reads \"punctate epithelial keratitis, **pseudodendritic ulcer**, mucous plaques, disciform keratitis, neurotrophic keratopathy\". On the deck's wording the word *typical* is wrong. **The key stands as printed** — and A survives because the other three options are each contradicted outright, so A is the only one left standing.\n\n**Why each of the other three is false, against the deck:**\n\n| Option | What the deck says |\n|---|---|\n| **The associated keratitis has no tendency to recur.** | Zoster eye disease is notoriously **relapsing** — the deck lists \"Stromal / disciform keratitis\" and \"**Neurotrophic keratopathy**\" among long-term HZO complications, along with \"corneal anaesthesia\", and disciform disease in particular flares repeatedly as steroids are tapered. |\n| **Topical acyclovir is sufficient to treat associated keratitis.** | Flatly contradicted: \"Treatment: **oral valaciclovir/famciclovir within 72 h** of rash onset\". HZO is a **systemic** antiviral disease — topical alone is not treatment, and the 72-hour window is the examinable number. |\n| **Paralytic strabismus is very unlikely to occur.** | Zoster can involve the third, fourth and sixth cranial nerves and produce an ophthalmoplegia. *(This specific complication is **not named in `L15,16)`**, whose HZO complication list is ocular-surface and neuralgic; it is standard and uncontested, and the option's \"very unlikely\" is what makes it wrong.)* |\n\n**The rest of the HZO complication list**, printed: \"Epithelial keratitis · Stromal / disciform keratitis · Neurotrophic keratopathy · **Scleritis / episcleritis** · **Post-herpetic neuralgia**\" — the last of which is the commonest and most miserable long-term outcome, and the reason antivirals are started fast.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Vesicles in the V1 dermatome with any involvement of the **tip of the nose** buy the patient an ophthalmic examination and **oral** antiviral within 72 hours. Then check corneal sensation, because a numb cornea after zoster is what loses these eyes years later.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Herpes zoster ophthalmicus follows the V1 dermatome; it needs ORAL antiviral within 72 h, recurs, and its corneal lesion is a stellate pseudodendrite without terminal buds — this bank calls it a typical dendrite.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.48 (Q324), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-325', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'Regarding **keratoconus**:',
    options:['It presents a painless progressive diminution of vision though rapid drop of vision may occur.','Manson’s sign occurs early in the disease.','Pachymetry shows an increased corneal thickness.','Soft contact lenses may be used in moderate cases'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Keratoconus is a slow, painless, bilateral loss of vision — with one exception, and the option is careful to include it.** The slow part is the ectasia itself: `L15,16) Cornea`, \"**Progressive bilateral (often asymmetric) corneal thinning and protrusion** · Onset: **puberty**; progression until **3rd–4th decade**\". The exception is **acute hydrops**: \"sudden stromal oedema from **Descemet's rupture**\" — a break in Descemet's lets aqueous flood the stroma, the cornea whitens overnight and vision falls off a cliff, with discomfort and watering. That is the \"rapid drop of vision may occur\" clause, and it is the event described in the vignette at `opqb-t8-340`.\n\n**Why the other three are false:**\n\n| Option | What the deck actually says |\n|---|---|\n| **Manson’s sign occurs early in the disease.** | Two errors. The name is **Munson's** — the bank misprints it here and spells it correctly at `opqb-t8-313` and `opqb-t8-314`. And the sign is **late**: \"**Munson's sign: V-shaped deformity of lower lid on downgaze**\" requires a cone protruding far enough to indent the lid margin, which is advanced disease. |\n| **Pachymetry shows an increased corneal thickness.** | The opposite. Ectasia is defined by \"progressive corneal **thinning** & protrusion\", and the deck's screening thresholds are all minima — \"Central thickness ≥500 µm\", \"RSB <300 µm = absolute contraindication\". Pachymetry in keratoconus shows **thinning**, maximal at the cone. |\n| **Soft contact lenses may be used in moderate cases** | The deck's ladder does list \"Soft / rigid contact lenses\" together at rung 2 — but a **soft** lens drapes over the cone and reproduces its irregular surface, so it corrects only the spherical part and leaves the irregular astigmatism uncorrected. This bank keys precisely that at `opqb-t8-335`: advanced keratoconus can NOT be managed by soft contact lenses. Soft lenses have a place in **early, mild** disease with little irregularity, not in moderate or advanced disease. |\n\n**⚠️ Note the tension between the bank and the deck on option D**, recorded rather than resolved: the deck groups soft and rigid lenses on one rung, whereas this bank (here and at `opqb-t8-335`) treats soft lenses as inadequate. The bank's position is the clinically standard one and both of its keys are internally consistent.\n\n**The rest of the picture:** risk factors \"**eye rubbing**, atopy, Down syndrome, connective tissue disorders\"; signs Vogt's striae and Munson's; topography \"**inferior steepening, asymmetric bowtie**\"; management spectacles → contact lenses → scleral lenses → **CXL** → ICRS → keratoplasty; and the instruction that ends the panel, \"**Avoid eye rubbing!**\"\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Painless, bilateral, adolescent-onset, un-correctable astigmatism is keratoconus until topography says otherwise — and if it is progressing, **cross-linking now** is worth more than a better pair of lenses, because CXL \"does NOT improve vision – it **stabilizes progression**\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Keratoconus is painless and progressive, with sudden visual loss only in acute hydrops; Munson’s sign is LATE, pachymetry shows THINNING, and soft lenses cannot correct the irregular astigmatism.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.48 (Q325), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-326', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Local steroids** are contraindicated in the case of **bacterial** corneal ulcer for fear of:',
    options:['Secondary glaucoma','Cortical cataract','Corneal perforation','Secondary viral infection'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The bacterial version of `opqb-t8-301`, with the same four options and the same key — and the deck gives it its own panel.** `L15,16) Cornea`, *Common Prescribing Errors*, under **Topical Steroids in Bacterial Keratitis**: \"**Can potentiate infection · Delay healing · Wait until infection is controlled · Then use judiciously under ophthalmologist supervision**\". Its bacterial keratitis treatment slide states the rule directly: \"**AVOID topical steroids until infectious cause is excluded or controlled**\".\n\n**How that ends in a hole.** A steroid does two things at once to an infected cornea: it suppresses the local immune response, so the organism multiplies unopposed, and it inhibits collagen synthesis by keratocytes, so the stroma cannot repair the damage being done. Melting accelerates, and the endpoint is **perforation**. The deck supplies the speed at which this can happen with the worst organism: \"**Pseudomonas can cause rapid descemetocele → perforation within 24–48 h**\" — and that is *without* a steroid helping it.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it is not the fear here |\n|---|---|\n| **Secondary glaucoma** | Steroid-response ocular hypertension is real, dose- and duration-dependent, and reversible on stopping. It is a reason to monitor pressure in anyone on long-term steroid — not the reason to withhold the drop from an infected cornea today. |\n| **Cortical cataract** | Steroid cataract is **posterior subcapsular**, not cortical, and takes months to years. Wrong morphology and wrong timescale. |\n| **Secondary viral infection** | Reads plausibly — immunosuppression does permit opportunistic infection, and steroid use is listed by the deck as a **risk factor for fungal** keratitis (\"Candida: immunocompromised, contact lens wearers, **topical steroids**\"; \"Fungal Keratitis: … Topical steroids history\"). But the fear that governs the contraindication is structural loss of the cornea, not a superadded virus. |\n\n**When steroids do become appropriate**, so the rule is not learned as an absolute: once the organism is identified and the infection is demonstrably controlled on antibiotic, a steroid can reduce the scarring that would otherwise sit on the visual axis — the deck's own words, \"**Then use judiciously under ophthalmologist supervision**\". That decision belongs to the ophthalmologist with the slit lamp, not to the first prescriber.\n\n**⚠️ Sibling.** `opqb-t8-301` asks the identical question about a **viral** ulcer, with the same options and the same key letter. The mechanisms differ — viral replication versus bacterial potentiation — but the feared endpoint is the same.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** No steroid drop should ever be given to a red eye that has not been stained with fluorescein and examined at a slit lamp. That single discipline prevents most of the corneas lost to this error.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Steroids in a bacterial corneal ulcer potentiate the infection and delay healing, so the fear is stromal melting and CORNEAL PERFORATION; steroid glaucoma and PSC cataract are slow, reversible, secondary worries.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.48 (Q326), key p.53' },

  /* opqb-t8-327 WAS FOLDED, NOT WRITTEN -- 2026-08-24.
     It is a WITHIN-BANK REPRINT of `opqb-t2-77` (topic 2, book p.9): same 46 D
     vertical against 45 D horizontal keratometry, same emmetropic retinoscopy,
     the same four options in the same order, the same key, the same chapter.
     A within-bank reprint takes NO `alsoIn` -- it is held once.
     TWO CONSEQUENCES WERE APPLIED TO THE HELD ENTRY, not just a deletion:
       - p.49/p.53 PRINTS AN EXPLANATION BOX and p.14 did not, so the box was
         folded into `opqb-t2-77` verbatim, and
       - its AUTHORED MARKER CAME OFF, because the bank does print an
         explanation for this question after all.
     Its `source` now names both printings. */
  { id:'opqb-t8-328', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Patching the eye** is contraindicated in',
    options:['After glaucoma surgery','Bacterial keratitis','Corneal epithelial erosions','Allergic keratitis'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A pad over an infected cornea makes a warm, dark, moist, closed pocket — which is a culture medium — and it hides the eye from you at the moment you most need to watch it.** An infective keratitis needs frequent drops (the deck's regimen is \"intensive topical fluoroquinolones… **hourly day 1**\") and daily re-examination, and a patch prevents both.\n\n**⚠️ How this is grounded.** `L15,16) Cornea` never uses the word *patch*. What it does prescribe, and what is incompatible with an occlusive pad, is printed: hourly topical antibiotic, corneal scraping before treatment, and admission criteria that turn on watching the ulcer — \"Admit if: central ulcer >2 mm, threat of perforation, poor compliance, or **no improvement in 48 h**\". *(The specific contraindication to patching an infected or contact-lens-related keratitis is therefore **not taken from the course material**; it is standard, uncontested practice and is given because the bank asks it. Measured: *patching* and *pad* return no teaching hit in any cached ophthalmology deck or book chapter.)*\n\n**Why the other three tolerate or benefit from a pad or shield:**\n\n| Option | Why patching is acceptable there |\n|---|---|\n| **After glaucoma surgery** | The eye is routinely padded and **shielded** after intraocular surgery to protect a fresh wound and a shallow chamber, usually overnight. |\n| **Corneal epithelial erosions** | The classical indication. A clean, uninfected abrasion is comfortable under a pad, and the deck's own trauma line — \"Corneal abrasion: fluorescein staining delineates defect; treat with topical antibiotic ± cycloplegic; **usually heals in 24–48 h**\" — describes exactly the lesion for which patching or a **bandage contact lens** is used. This bank endorses a bandage in an ulcer at `opqb-t8-315`: \"Bandage → sometimes used to protect the cornea and reduce pain\". |\n| **Allergic keratitis** | Not an infection. A pad is not usually needed, but nothing about it is dangerous. The weakest distractor. |\n\n**⚠️ A tension inside this bank, recorded not resolved.** `opqb-t8-315` lists **bandage** as an acceptable part of treating a *simple* corneal ulcer, while this question keys patching as contraindicated in *bacterial* keratitis. The two are reconcilable — a **bandage contact lens** on a clean epithelial defect is not the same intervention as an **occlusive pad** over an infected, discharging cornea — but a reader who does not draw that distinction will see a contradiction.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** If it is infected or discharging, leave it open, treat it hourly, and look at it again tomorrow. If it is a clean abrasion, a pad or a bandage lens is kindness.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Never patch an infected cornea — a pad incubates the organism and hides an ulcer that needs hourly drops and daily review; patching belongs to clean epithelial defects.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.49 (Q328), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-329', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Efferent** in corneal sensitivity testing is',
    options:['Oculomotor','Facial','Nasocilliary','Long ciliary'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Testing corneal sensation is testing the blink reflex, and a reflex has two limbs. The AFFERENT limb is the trigeminal nerve; the EFFERENT limb — the one that actually closes the lids — is the FACIAL nerve.** The question asks for the efferent, so the answer is VII.\n\n| Limb | Nerve | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Afferent** | **V1** (ophthalmic division of trigeminal), via the **long ciliary** and **nasociliary** branches | Carries the sensation from the cornea |\n| **Efferent** | **CN VII (facial)** | Contracts **orbicularis oculi**, closing both eyes — the response you look for |\n\n**What the material supplies, and what it does not.** `L15,16) Cornea` gives the afferent side unambiguously — \"Most densely innervated tissue in the body (**trigeminal nerve, ophthalmic division**)\", \"Innervation: **CN V1 (ophthalmic)**\" — and it gives the facial nerve its motor role in the same deck, under exposure keratopathy: \"Causes: **VII nerve palsy (Bell's)**, thyroid eye disease, proptosis\", which is a palsy of lid **closure**. *(The blink reflex as a named two-limb arc is **not taken from the course material**; no cached ophthalmology deck or book chapter sets out its afferent and efferent limbs. The assignment above is standard and uncontested, and it is exactly what the option list is built on.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Oculomotor** | CN III — motor to four extraocular muscles and **levator palpebrae** (lid *opening*), plus parasympathetic supply to the pupil sphincter and ciliary muscle. It **raises** the lid; it cannot close it. A levator palsy therefore causes ptosis, which this bank keys as *protective* at `opqb-t8-320`. |\n| **Nasocilliary** | A branch of **V1** — so it is on the **afferent** side, and printed here with a double L. The most tempting wrong answer, because it is genuinely the nerve that carries corneal sensation and is the branch behind Hutchinson's sign (`opqb-t8-343`). Wrong limb of the arc. |\n| **Long ciliary** | The terminal branches of the nasociliary that actually enter the globe and innervate the cornea. Again **afferent**. |\n\n*(Options C and D being the same pathway at two levels is a hint in itself: when two distractors are the same answer, neither can be the key.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Because the two limbs are different nerves, the test **localises** as well as detects. Touch the cornea and watch **both** eyes: if neither blinks, the afferent (V1) is out — a numb cornea, which is the dangerous finding. If the tested eye does not close but the other one does, the efferent (VII) is out on that side — a Bell's palsy, and the risk is **exposure**, not anaesthesia. The two need opposite kinds of vigilance.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Corneal sensitivity testing is the blink reflex: afferent V1 (nasociliary/long ciliary), efferent CN VII (facial, orbicularis oculi). Watching both eyes localises which limb has failed.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.49 (Q329), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-330', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The photograph shows one eye with the lids retracted. **The cornea is diffusely hazy across its whole diameter — a blue-grey ground-glass veil through which no iris detail and no pupil margin can be seen. The conjunctiva is white and quiet, with no injection, no discharge and no discrete ulcer or infiltrate.** What is the cause of the sign being shown?',
    options:['Viral','Eye misalignment','Breaks in desement membrane','Allergy'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ THIS QUESTION HAS A FIGURE and the bank's printed stem is only \"What is the cause of the sign being shown?\" — it names no finding at all, so it cannot be answered without the photograph. The stem above restates **what is visible** in the printed image so that the entry stands alone. The image crop does not exist yet; the coordinates and the alt-text caution are recorded in `gg-t8.array.js`. Nothing in the restatement names the diagnosis or the mechanism, because doing so would answer the question.)*\n\n**A cornea that is uniformly cloudy in a quiet, uninflamed eye has lost its DETURGESCENCE — the water it is holding is what scatters the light.** Deturgescence is maintained by one structure and one only: `L15,16) Cornea`, \"**Endothelium is critical for maintaining transparency via active fluid pumping**\", \"Endothelium: single cell layer, **Na/K ATPase pump** · **Cannot regenerate**\". Anything that tears the endothelium and its basement membrane, **Descemet's**, lets aqueous into the stroma and the cornea goes grey.\n\n**The deck gives that exact event a name.** In the keratoconus panel: \"**Acute hydrops: sudden stromal oedema from Descemet's rupture**\" — and this bank builds the vignette of a resolved hydrops at `opqb-t8-340`. The other classical route to breaks in Descemet's is **buphthalmos**, where a stretching infant globe splits the membrane and leaves horizontal or curvilinear scars; those breaks likewise flood the stroma and cloud the cornea. Either way the answer to *what caused this haze* is a break in Descemet's membrane. *(The buphthalmos route, and the eponym for those linear scars, are **not taken from `L15,16) Cornea`** — its own Descemet's-rupture teaching is the keratoconus one. Congenital glaucoma is taught in `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry`.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and each is excluded by something visible in the picture:**\n\n| Option | What it would look like instead |\n|---|---|\n| **Viral** | Herpetic disease gives a **discrete** lesion — a branching dendrite, a geographic ulcer, or a **central disc** of oedema with keratic precipitates under it — in an eye with ciliary injection. This cornea is uniformly hazy end to end, with no lesion and no injection. |\n| **Eye misalignment** | Squint changes where the eye points; it does not change what the cornea transmits. The photograph shows one eye and no fixation target, and misalignment has no corneal sign at all. A pure filler. |\n| **Allergy** | Vernal and atopic disease give **papillae, chemosis, discharge, limbal Horner-Trantas dots** and an itchy, injected eye — sometimes a shield ulcer. This conjunctiva is white and quiet. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Read a cloudy cornea by asking which condition of transparency has failed (`opqb-t8-311`, `opqb-t8-331`). **Diffuse, whole-cornea haze in a quiet eye = the pump has failed** — Descemet's break, endothelial dystrophy, bullous keratopathy, or an acute pressure rise (`opqb-t8-342`). **A localised opacity in an inflamed eye = infiltrate, ulcer or scar.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapters: Glaucoma 2 — the buphthalmos route to Descemet's breaks — and Cornea, ectasia and grafts — the acute-hydrops route.)*",
    objective:'Diffuse whole-cornea haze in a quiet white eye means lost deturgescence: a break in Descemet’s membrane (acute hydrops, or buphthalmos) lets aqueous flood the stroma.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.49 (Q330, figure printed), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-331', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The factors of **corneal transparency** do **not** include:',
    options:['The epithelium is stratified squamous non keratinized.','The stroma is formed of fine collagenous fibers of the same refractive index.','The cornea is deturgescent.','The cornea is avascular; the nerve endings are unmyelinated.','The cornea contains high population of goblet cells.'],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**Goblet cells belong to the CONJUNCTIVA, not the cornea — and they would be an obstacle to transparency, not a condition of it.** They are mucin-secreting cells scattered through the conjunctival epithelium, densest medially and in the fornices, and their product is the innermost layer of the tear film (`opqb-t8-332`). The corneal epithelium is a plain **stratified squamous non-keratinised** sheet: `L15,16) Cornea`, \"5–6 cell layers thick · Regenerates completely in 5–7 days · **Tight junctions → barrier function** · Stains with fluorescein when disrupted\". No glands, no goblet cells, no vessels.\n\n**The four genuine conditions, which are options A–D:**\n\n| Factor | What `L15,16) Cornea` prints |\n|---|---|\n| **Non-keratinised epithelium** (A) | A smooth, unkeratinised surface; keratin would scatter light |\n| **Uniform stromal architecture** (B) | \"**Orthogonally arranged collagen lamellae**\" — 90 % of corneal thickness, with fibrils of uniform diameter and spacing so that scattered light cancels by interference |\n| **Deturgescence** (C) | \"**Endothelium is critical for maintaining transparency via active fluid pumping**\" — the Na/K ATPase keeps the stroma relatively dry. Lose it and the cornea clouds (`opqb-t8-330`, `opqb-t8-342`) |\n| **Avascular, unmyelinated nerves** (D) | \"**Completely avascular**; nutrients supplied by aqueous humour (posterior) and tears (anterior)\", and the nerves lose their myelin on entering — myelin, like vessels, would scatter |\n\n**⚠️ Sibling with a different exception.** `opqb-t8-311` asks the same content with **four** options and keys **myelinated nerve fibres**; here the same fact appears as part of the *correct* statement D, and the exception is the goblet cells instead. The underlying list is identical; only the wrong statement changes. Read the options.\n\n**Why the goblet-cell option is nonetheless tempting.** Goblet cells matter enormously to the cornea — mucin deficiency destabilises the tear film, and an unstable tear film gives punctate keratitis and a rough, dull surface (`opqb-t8-333`). But they act **on** the cornea from outside it; they are not in it.\n\n*(The optical physics of lamellar interference is **not taken from the course material**, which gives the anatomy and the pump but not the mechanism; the conjunctival distribution of goblet cells comes from `L9) lacrimal system.`'s secretory-part figure and from book ch.4, not from the cornea deck.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Five conditions, five diseases: keratinisation (severe dry eye, vitamin A deficiency) · disordered lamellae (scar) · oedema (endothelial failure, pressure rise) · vessels (chronic hypoxia, contact lens overwear) · deposits (dystrophy, band keratopathy). Naming which one has failed is usually the whole diagnosis.\n\n*(Five options, A–E, as printed; option B is set with the bank's justified spacing.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Corneal transparency rests on a non-keratinised epithelium, uniform stromal lamellae, deturgescence by the endothelial pump, avascularity and unmyelinated nerves — goblet cells are conjunctival.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.49 (Q331), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-332', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The cornea is covered by the **tear film**, which is formed of 3 components arranged in the following order **from inwards to outwards**:',
    options:['Lipid, mucous and watery.','Watery, mucous and lipid.','Mucous, watery and lipid.','Mucous, lipid and watery.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Read the direction of travel in the stem: *inwards to outwards* means starting at the cornea and finishing at the air.** From the epithelium outwards the order is **mucin → aqueous → lipid**.\n\n| Layer, from the cornea out | Made by | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mucin** (innermost) | **Conjunctival goblet cells** | Wets the naturally hydrophobic epithelium so the aqueous can spread — without it the tear film beads up and breaks |\n| **Aqueous** (the bulk) | **Main and accessory lacrimal glands** (Krause, Wolfring) | Nutrition, oxygen, lysozyme and immunoglobulin; the layer Schirmer's test measures |\n| **Lipid** (outermost) | **Meibomian glands** in the tarsal plates | Retards evaporation and seals the lid margin |\n\nThe mnemonic that survives exam pressure: **M-A-L, from the cornea to the air** — Mucin, Aqueous, Lipid. Reverse it and you have the order looking in from outside.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong** — they are the same three layers permuted, and each puts the wrong substance against the wrong interface:\n\n| Option | The impossibility |\n|---|---|\n| **Lipid, mucous and watery.** | Puts **oil against the epithelium**, which would repel the tears rather than hold them, and leaves the watery layer exposed to the air to evaporate freely. The exact inverse of the truth. |\n| **Watery, mucous and lipid.** | Puts water directly on a hydrophobic surface with the wetting agent stranded in the middle. The mucin has to be innermost for the film to adhere at all. |\n| **Mucous, lipid and watery.** | Gets the innermost layer right, then leaves the **aqueous exposed** to the air with the oil buried under it — no evaporation barrier. The best distractor, because it starts correctly. |\n\n**What the material supplies.** The cornea deck names the tear film only as a pre-refractive-surgery check — \"Adequate tear film (**Schirmer's, TBUT**)\". The three-layer scheme and its glands are taught in the **lacrimal** material: book ch.4 p.63 makes the secretory system \"the **main lacrimal gland**, the **accessory lacrimal glands**, **conjunctival goblet cells** and the **Meibomian glands**\", and `L9) lacrimal system.` slides 4 and 5 label \"The Secretory Part / Aqueous part\" and \"The Secretory Part / **Mucin and Lipid** part\". *(The inward-to-outward ordering itself is **not printed as a sequence** in any cached source — the sources give the layers and their glands, not their stacking order. The order above is standard and uncontested.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Each layer fails differently and is treated differently. **Aqueous deficiency** — Sjögren, ageing — shows a low Schirmer and wants substitution or punctal plugs. **Lipid deficiency** — meibomian gland dysfunction, blepharitis, by far the commonest — shows a short **TBUT** with a normal Schirmer and wants lid hygiene and warm compresses. **Mucin deficiency** — vitamin A deficiency, cicatrising conjunctivitis — destroys wetting itself. Deciding which layer is failing is what makes dry eye treatable.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Lacrimal System — the tear film and its glands are taught in `op-lac`, but the question is asked about the corneal surface.)*",
    objective:'The tear film from the cornea outwards is MUCIN (goblet cells) → AQUEOUS (lacrimal glands) → LIPID (meibomian glands); each layer fails in a different disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.49 (Q332), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-333', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The **corneal luster** is due to the integrity of the **tear film** accompanied by:',
    options:['Intact epithelium.','Intact Bowman’s membrane.','Intact Descemet’s membrane.','Intact endothelium.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Luster is a surface property — it is light reflected specularly off the front of the eye — so it depends on the two structures at the front: the tear film and the epithelium it lies on.** A smooth, unbroken epithelium gives the tear film something to spread evenly over; break the epithelium and the film breaks up over the defect, the reflected image of the light source becomes distorted or dull, and the cornea loses its shine. That is the same physics the fluorescein test exploits from the other direction (`opqb-t8-299`).\n\n`L15,16) Cornea` describes the epithelium in exactly the terms that make it the surface partner of the tear film: \"5–6 cell layers thick · Regenerates completely in 5–7 days · **Tight junctions → barrier function** · Stains with fluorescein when disrupted\". *(The word *luster* itself appears in no cached ophthalmology deck or book chapter — measured across all 29 decks and 12 book chapters. The reasoning above is standard clinical optics and is **not taken from the course material**; what the material supplies is the anatomy of the two structures involved.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — all three are too deep to be seen from the front:**\n\n| Option | Where it sits, and what its loss actually does |\n|---|---|\n| **Intact Bowman’s membrane.** | 12 µm of \"acellular, condensed collagen\" **under** the epithelium. \"**Does NOT regenerate if damaged**\" — so damaging it causes permanent **scarring**, not loss of shine. PRK ablates directly onto it and the eye still shines afterwards. |\n| **Intact Descemet’s membrane.** | The basement membrane of the endothelium, at the **back**. A break in it floods the stroma and clouds the whole cornea (`opqb-t8-330`) — the cornea goes grey, which is a transparency failure, not a surface one. |\n| **Intact endothelium.** | The deepest layer of all, a single sheet with a Na/K ATPase pump. Its failure causes **oedema** — Fuchs' dystrophy, bullous keratopathy — and again clouds the cornea from within rather than dulling its surface. |\n\n**The distinction the question is really drawing: LUSTER versus TRANSPARENCY.**\n\n| | Depends on | Lost in |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Luster** (shine) | Tear film + **epithelium** | Dry eye, exposure, abrasion, keratitis, vitamin A deficiency |\n| **Transparency** (clarity) | Stromal regularity, avascularity, **deturgescence** | Oedema, scar, dystrophy, vascularisation, deposits |\n\nA cornea can be perfectly transparent and utterly dull (early dry eye), or bright and shiny and completely opaque (an old flat scar).\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Loss of corneal luster is one of the earliest signs available without a slit lamp — a pen torch and a window reflection will show it. In a child it should prompt thought about **vitamin A deficiency and xerophthalmia**; in an adult, about dry eye, exposure or an epithelial defect. Do not wait for the cornea to go cloudy.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Corneal luster is a surface phenomenon — an intact tear film spread over an intact EPITHELIUM; Bowman’s, Descemet’s and the endothelium govern transparency instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.49 (Q333), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-334', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'A **school boy 10 years old**, complains of blurring of vision. **uncomfortable glasses, frequent itchy sensation**. O/E: vision with his glasses was **6/60 and 6/36**. **It was not possible to have accurate refraction.** The cornea looks clear, but the **right anterior chamber looks deeper than the left**. The main cause of defective vision may be:',
    options:['Active trachoma.','Bulbar spring catarrh.','Early keratoconus.','High myopia.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Four separate clues in this stem all point one way, and the strongest of them is the sentence that says the refraction could not be pinned down.**\n\n| Clue in the stem | What `L15,16) Cornea` prints |\n|---|---|\n| **10-year-old, blurring** | \"Onset: **puberty**; progression until 3rd–4th decade\" |\n| **Glasses never comfortable, acuity poor with them, refraction not obtainable** | Ectasias \"All cause **irregular astigmatism → reduced best spectacle-corrected visual acuity**\". An irregular cornea cannot be neutralised by a spectacle lens, so the refractionist gets a different answer every time and the boy never sees well |\n| **Frequent itchy sensation** | \"Risk factors: **eye rubbing, atopy**, Down syndrome, connective tissue disorders\" — itching means atopy, atopy means rubbing, and rubbing is the mechanical driver of the ectasia. The panel ends \"**Avoid eye rubbing!**\" |\n| **One anterior chamber deeper than the other** | The cone protrudes forward, carrying the corneal apex away from the iris — so the chamber is **deeper** on the worse side. It also fits \"Progressive **bilateral (often asymmetric)**\" |\n\nNote that the cornea is described as **clear**: this is *early* keratoconus, before Vogt's striae, hydrops scarring or Munson's sign. The diagnosis at this stage is made by **topography**, not by looking — \"Topography: **inferior steepening, asymmetric bowtie pattern**\", and it is \"sensitive to very early, subclinical irregularities\" (`opqb-t8-341`).\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Active trachoma.** | A chronic cicatrising **conjunctival** infection. It would give follicles, papillae and superior pannus, and in an active stage a visibly abnormal cornea and tarsal conjunctiva — not a clear cornea with an unobtainable refraction. It also does not deepen an anterior chamber. |\n| **Bulbar spring catarrh.** | Vernal keratoconjunctivitis — and the closest trap, because it explains the **itching** perfectly and is a genuine disease of boys this age. But it does not by itself make the refraction unobtainable or deepen a chamber. Its real relevance is as the **cause upstream**: chronic itching → chronic rubbing → keratoconus, and the deck names atopy as a keratoconus risk factor for exactly this reason. |\n| **High myopia.** | Would give poor **uncorrected** vision but should correct well with glasses, and the refraction would be measurable and stable. High myopia deepens the anterior chamber too, but bilaterally and in proportion to a measurable error — and the stem says the glasses do not work. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The examinable pattern is *a young patient whose glasses never work and whose prescription keeps changing*. Do not order another refraction — order a **topography**. If it is progressing, cross-linking now protects the vision he has, because CXL \"does NOT improve vision – it **stabilizes progression**\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Conjunctiva — vernal disease is taught there, and is the mechanism by which the rubbing starts.)*",
    objective:'A child with itching, glasses that never work and an unobtainable refraction has early keratoconus — irregular astigmatism from an ectatic cornea, diagnosed by topography while the cornea still looks clear.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.50 (Q334), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-335', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'**Advanced Keratoconus** can **NOT** be managed by:',
    options:['Rigid gas permeable contact lenses.','Soft contact lenses.','Penetrating keratoplasty.','Hard contact lens.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A soft lens is too flexible to help. It drapes over the cone, takes up the cornea's own irregular shape, and hands that irregularity straight back to the eye — so it corrects the spherical error and leaves the irregular astigmatism untouched.** `L15,16) Cornea` states the problem the lens has to solve: the ectasias \"All cause **irregular astigmatism → reduced best spectacle-corrected visual acuity**\". Only a lens that keeps its own shape can present a new, regular front surface, with the tear film filling the space behind it.\n\n**Why the other three do work in advanced disease:**\n\n| Option | Why it manages an advanced cone |\n|---|---|\n| **Rigid gas permeable contact lenses.** | The deck's rung 2 — a rigid front surface plus a tear lens neutralises the irregularity, with enough oxygen transmission for daily wear. This is the lens the patient in `opqb-t8-340` is wearing at 6/9. |\n| **Penetrating keratoplasty.** | Rung 6 — replaces the cornea outright. The deck prefers **DALK** where possible (\"Preserves host endothelium… **No endothelial rejection risk**; lower overall rejection\", \"Preferred for keratoconus\") and reserves PKP for \"scarring, perforations, failed DALK\" — but PKP certainly manages the disease, and this bank keys it at `opqb-t8-340`. |\n| **Hard contact lens.** | The same rigid optics as RGP, in the older PMMA material. Options A and D are the same principle twice, which is itself a hint that neither can be the exception. |\n\n**⚠️ Its sibling has a DIFFERENT key, and the difference is only in what is offered.** `opqb-t8-305` asks the near-identical question — advanced keratoconus, **least** corrected by — but offers **spectacles** in place of soft lenses, and keys spectacles. Both keys follow one principle: **whatever is too flexible or too far away to impose a regular front surface fails.** A spectacle lens sits 12 mm from a cornea it cannot reshape; a soft lens sits on the cornea and copies it. Read the option list before answering either.\n\n**⚠️ A tension with the deck, recorded and not corrected.** The deck's management ladder groups them — \"2. **Soft / rigid** contact lenses\" — without saying that soft lenses fail in advanced disease. This bank draws the distinction the deck does not, here and at `opqb-t8-325` (where \"Soft contact lenses may be used in moderate cases\" is keyed **false**). The bank's position is the clinically standard one and it is internally consistent across all three questions.\n\n**The full ladder, for reference:** spectacles (early, mild) → soft/rigid contact lenses → **scleral lenses** (\"advanced astigmatism\") → **CXL** (UV-A + riboflavin, to stabilise) → **ICRS/Intacs** → **keratoplasty: DALK (preferred) or PKP**.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In advanced keratoconus the question is not *which lens is most comfortable* but *which lens can hold its shape against the cone* — and when none can, the answer is a graft.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Soft contact lenses conform to the cone and cannot correct irregular astigmatism, so they fail in advanced keratoconus; rigid/hard lenses and keratoplasty both work.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.50 (Q335), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-336', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'What is meant by **LASIK**, which means changing the refractive power of the eye by:',
    options:['Flattening of the cornea, by making radial incisions.','Flattening of the cornea, by inserting an intra corneal ring.','Flattening of the corneas, by means of excimer laser photo ablation of superficial stromal layer not interfering with Bowman membrane.','Flattening of the cornea, by removal of the superficial stromal layers.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**LASIK is *laser-assisted in situ keratomileusis*: a flap is lifted, an excimer laser ablates the stroma underneath it, and the flap is laid back down.** `L15,16) Cornea` defines it twice — in the box this bank reprints at `opqb-t8-319`, \"**LASIK (Laser-assisted in situ keratomileusis): Laser ablates corneal stroma under flap**\", and in its comparison table: \"**Flap created** (microkeratome / femtosecond) · **Excimer laser ablates exposed stroma** · Fast recovery (1–2 days) · Risk: flap dislocation, **ectasia**, DLK ('sands of Sahara') · Wide range: myopia up to **−10 D**\".\n\n**Why option C is the one that describes it, including its odd final clause.** \"Not interfering with Bowman membrane\" is what distinguishes LASIK from **PRK**: in LASIK the epithelium and Bowman's layer are lifted **within the flap** and replaced intact, so the ablation happens in the stroma beneath them; in PRK the epithelium is scraped off and \"**ablation directly on Bowman's**\". That is precisely why PRK hurts for three to five days and can haze, while LASIK recovers in one to two.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — each is a real procedure, just not this one:**\n\n| Option | What it actually is |\n|---|---|\n| **Radial incisions** | **Radial keratotomy (RK)** — the pre-laser operation, deep radial cuts that flatten the central cornea by weakening the periphery. Abandoned for unpredictable and progressive hyperopic drift. *(RK is **not named in the course material**; no cached deck or book chapter mentions it. Given because the bank offers it.)* |\n| **Intra corneal ring** | **ICRS / Intacs** — the deck's rung 5 in keratoconus management, \"Intracorneal ring segments (ICRS / Intacs)\", inserted into the stroma to flatten a cone. A treatment for ectasia, not a refractive procedure for a normal eye. |\n| **Removal of the superficial stromal layers** | Closest to **PRK** (\"Epithelium removed; ablation directly on Bowman's\") or to **PTK** (\"Laser removes superficial corneal opacities\"). Superficial, and with no flap — which is exactly the difference. The best distractor. |\n\n**Two newer relatives the deck teaches**, worth attaching here: **FemtoLASIK**, which uses a femtosecond laser rather than a blade for the flap — \"more precise, thinner flap · Reduces flap complications · no blade used\" — and **SMILE**, which has no flap at all: \"Femtosecond laser cuts lenticule **inside intact cornea** · Removed via 4 mm incision · **Less corneal nerve damage → less dry eye** · Lower infection/ectasia risk\".\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Every LASIK decision is a thickness calculation before it is an optical one: the flap plus the ablation must leave a **residual stromal bed ≥300 µm**, or the cornea will ectasia later (`opqb-t8-316`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'LASIK = a corneal flap plus excimer ablation of the stroma beneath it, leaving epithelium and Bowman’s intact within the flap; PRK ablates directly on Bowman’s after removing the epithelium.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.50 (Q336), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-337', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'A **16-year-old female** wearing glasses of **-4.00 D** in both eyes came asking you whether she should do an excimer laser surgery **(LASIK)** to correct her myopia. **Her last year glasses were -3.00D.** She uses a soft contact lens occasionally with no complications. Her **best-corrected visual acuity is 6/6** in both eyes. Which statement is **contra indicated** in this case?',
    options:['No Lasik except after examining corneal thickness.','Do LASIK when she is 18 years old.','Do LASIK at once','Must not do LASIK except when her refraction is stable for one year after full investigations'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**LASIK is not recommended in patients with unstable refraction (progressing myopia)**\nUsually performed **after 18 years** old when refraction stabilizes with full preoperative investigations, including corneal thickness and topography\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Two facts in the stem forbid operating today: she is 16, and her myopia moved a whole dioptre in a year.** The stem asks which statement is *contra indicated* — that is, which course of action must not be taken — so the answer is the one that says operate now.\n\n`L15,16) Cornea` supplies both thresholds. Age: \"Age requirement: typically **≥18–21 years** with stable refraction\", and its checklist says \"✓ **Age ≥21 years**\". Stability: \"Mandatory pre-op assessment: corneal topography, pachymetry, pupil size, tear film, **refraction stability (≥1 yr)**\", and among the contraindications, \"forme fruste keratoconus, thin cornea (<500 µm), **unstable refraction**, dry eye, active inflammation\".\n\n**Why operating on a moving refraction is not merely premature but harmful.** The laser corrects the error present on the day. If the eye goes on elongating, the myopia returns, and the cornea has already been thinned — so the retreatment that would fix it may be impossible, and the residual stromal bed may no longer be safe. That is the mechanism behind the deck's most feared complication: \"**Ectasia is the most feared long-term complication of LASIK**\", with risk factors \"forme fruste keratoconus, thin cornea, **young age**, high myopia, low residual stromal bed (RSB <300 µm)\". *Young age* is on that list for exactly this reason.\n\n**Why the other three are correct statements** — note the stem's inversion: the three wrong answers are the three *right* pieces of advice.\n\n| Option | Why it is sound advice |\n|---|---|\n| **No Lasik except after examining corneal thickness.** | Pachymetry is mandatory: \"<500 µm is a **relative** contraindication; RSB <300 µm = **absolute** contraindication for LASIK\". |\n| **Do LASIK when she is 18 years old.** | Matches the deck's \"typically ≥18–21 years with stable refraction\" and its own box, \"after 18 years old\". |\n| **Must not do LASIK except when her refraction is stable for one year after full investigations** | The stability rule and the full workup, stated together — the most complete and safest of the four. |\n\n**A note on her contact lenses**, which the stem mentions for a reason: the checklist says \"**Discontinue CLs before mapping**\" — a lens moulds the cornea, and topography taken too soon after wear can mimic or mask an irregularity.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Two consecutive stable refractions a year apart, in an adult, with a normal topography and adequate thickness. Anything less and the honest answer is *not yet* — and \"not yet\" costs this patient nothing but a pair of glasses.",
    objective:'LASIK requires refraction stable for ≥1 year and age ≥18–21, plus topography and pachymetry; operating on a progressing myope risks regression and post-LASIK ectasia.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.50 (Q337), key p.53 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-338', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'A **35-year-old man** complaining of pain in his right eye for several days, watering, photophobia, and blurring of the vision. **These symptoms are recurrent.** He also had **an acute attack of common cold the week before.** The most probable cause is:',
    options:['Catarrhal conjunctivitis','Chronic dacryocystitis.','Dendritic ulcer.','Acute angle closure glaucoma.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Two words in the stem make the diagnosis: *recurrent*, and *cold*.** Herpes simplex is latent in the trigeminal ganglion and reactivates when the host is stressed — a febrile illness being the classical trigger, and the reason the lay name for the labial form is a *cold sore*. `L15,16) Cornea` makes recurrence a defining feature: \"**Recurrent episodes**\" distinguishes HSV keratitis in its red-eye differential, and the deck prescribes against it — \"**Prophylactic oral aciclovir 400 mg twice daily reduces recurrence rate by ~50%**\". This bank keys recurrence directly at `opqb-t8-312` and `opqb-t8-344`.\n\nThe symptom set fits an epithelial corneal lesion exactly: unilateral pain, watering, photophobia and blurring, over days rather than hours. Confirm it in one move — **fluorescein and a cobalt blue light** — looking for the deck's \"linear branching lesion with **terminal buds**\", which it calls \"pathognomonic of HSV dendrite\". Treatment is a topical antiviral and **no steroid** (`opqb-t8-303`, `opqb-t8-301`).\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Catarrhal conjunctivitis** | A cold does cause conjunctivitis, so this is the trap the stem's last sentence sets. But conjunctivitis gives **grittiness and discharge**, not the pain, photophobia and blurred vision of a corneal lesion; it is usually **bilateral** or spreads to the second eye; and it does not recur in the same eye over years. |\n| **Chronic dacryocystitis.** | Presents with **epiphora and mucopurulent regurgitation** on pressing the sac, and a painless swelling at the inner canthus — not photophobia, not blurring, not recurrent attacks of pain. Wrong symptom set entirely. |\n| **Acute angle closure glaucoma.** | Genuinely painful with blurred vision — but it is an **emergency over hours**, not \"several days\", with haloes, nausea and vomiting, a hazy cornea, a fixed mid-dilated pupil and a stony-hard globe. And it does not recur weekly for years, nor follow a cold. Age 35 is also young for it. |\n\n**The differential that actually matters at the slit lamp**, once the dendrite is suspected: herpes **zoster** gives a **pseudo**dendrite — \"smaller, stellate, **lacks terminal buds**\" — and comes with a V1 rash (`opqb-t8-324`); a healing abrasion can leave a branching-looking track; and Acanthamoeba is \"often misdiagnosed initially as HSV\" and should be considered in any contact-lens wearer with pain out of proportion.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** *Recurrent unilateral painful red eye* is herpes simplex until fluorescein says otherwise — and the history of previous identical episodes is worth more than any single examination finding, because it also tells you the patient may need prophylaxis rather than another course of ointment.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Recurrent unilateral pain, watering and photophobia after a febrile illness is reactivated herpes simplex — a dendritic ulcer; stain with fluorescein and look for terminal buds.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.50 (Q338), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-339', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Fluorescein test** is **not useful** in the following condition:',
    options:['The diagnosis of corneal scar','The diagnosis of corneal ulcer','The diagnosis of keratitis.','The diagnosis of dry eve'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Fluorescein stains where the epithelium is MISSING. A corneal scar is covered by intact epithelium — the damage is in the stroma underneath — so it does not stain, and the test tells you nothing about it.** `L15,16) Cornea` gives the rule in the epithelium's own description: \"Tight junctions → **barrier function** · **Stains with fluorescein when disrupted**\". An old scar is healed; the barrier is restored; the dye runs off it.\n\n**Why the other three are all fluorescein-dependent diagnoses:**\n\n| Option | What the stain shows |\n|---|---|\n| **Corneal ulcer** | The defect itself. This bank calls a positive test the \"**sure diagnostic sign**\" of an ulcer at `opqb-t8-302`, and the deck stages severity by the size of what stains — \"Admit if: **central ulcer >2 mm**\". |\n| **Keratitis** | The **pattern** is the diagnosis: \"Fluorescein: **stains the ulcer bed**\" in herpes epithelial disease, with \"terminal buds\" pathognomonic; \"diffuse punctate epithelial erosions\" in the welder of `opqb-t8-348`; feathery satellites in fungal disease; \"Corneal abrasion: **fluorescein staining delineates defect**\" in trauma. |\n| **Dry eve** *(printed for \"dry eye\")* | **Tear film break-up time.** Instil fluorescein, ask the patient to blink once, then hold the eye open under cobalt blue and time the first dry spot. A TBUT under 10 seconds means an unstable film. The deck names TBUT as a pre-refractive-surgery check — \"Adequate tear film (**Schirmer's, TBUT**)\" — and this bank keyed the 10-second threshold in its lacrimal topic at `opqb-t4-162`, quoting the book: \"tear film breakup time (TBUT) normally more than10 seconds (if less, it is an indication of an unstable tear film)\". Dry eye also gives **punctate staining**, classically inferior or interpalpebral. |\n\n**So how IS a corneal scar assessed?** By **direct slit-lamp examination** — its depth, density and position relative to the visual axis — and by the acuity it costs. If it is dense and central it becomes a keratoplasty indication: the deck lists \"Optical: keratoconus, **scarring**, dystrophies, pseudophakic bullous keratopathy\" among graft indications, and it is also why a dense scar blocks the view of the fundus and forces a **B-scan** instead (`opqb-t8-345`). *(That fluorescein specifically fails on scars is **not stated in the course material**; it follows directly from the deck's own statement that the dye stains disrupted epithelium. Recorded as an inference, not a citation.)*\n\n**⚠️ One caveat worth knowing.** A scar can be **irregular** enough to pool dye in its surface furrows, and a scarred cornea is prone to **recurrent erosions** which do stain. Neither makes the scar itself a fluorescein diagnosis.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Read the test as a question about the **epithelium**, not about the cornea. Stains → the surface is broken, and the shape tells you why. Does not stain → the surface is intact, so whatever is wrong is deeper: scar, oedema, dystrophy, or an inflammation behind the cornea altogether.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Fluorescein stains epithelial defects, so it diagnoses ulcers, keratitis and (by TBUT and punctate staining) dry eye — but a healed corneal scar has intact epithelium and does not stain.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.50 (Q339), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-340', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'A patient known to have **keratoconus** using **gas permeable rigid contact lenses successfully with best corrected visual acuity 6/9** in both eyes. He developed **sudden drop of vision with discomfort and watering in the right eye**. **Ten weeks later the vision improved partially to 6/60** with correction, **with disappearance of the discomfort and watering**. The next treatment is:',
    options:['Continue on the gas permeable contact lens.','Shift to soft contact lenses.','LASIK.','Penetrating keratoplasty'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The stem describes an episode of acute hydrops that has resolved and left a scar.** `L15,16) Cornea` names the event in the keratoconus panel: \"**Acute hydrops: sudden stromal oedema from Descemet's rupture**\". Read the timeline against that definition:\n\n| Stage in the stem | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| **Sudden drop of vision with discomfort and watering** | Descemet's membrane ruptured; aqueous flooded the stroma; the cornea went white and the epithelium wept |\n| **Ten weeks later, discomfort and watering gone** | The endothelium has slid across the break and re-established the pump — the oedema has cleared |\n| **Vision only 6/60, from 6/9** | What is left is a **stromal scar** at the site, sitting on the visual axis. It is not oedema, and it will not clear |\n\n**Why that means a graft.** The eye's problem is no longer refractive and no longer treatable by anything that sits on the surface: a scar is opacity in the stroma, and no lens can see through it. The deck's graft indications name it — \"Optical: **keratoconus, scarring**, dystrophies, pseudophakic bullous keratopathy\" — and its management ladder ends there: \"6. **Keratoplasty: DALK (preferred) or PKP**\".\n\n**⚠️ Why the key is PENETRATING keratoplasty rather than DALK, even though the deck prefers DALK.** DALK \"Removes epithelium + stroma **down to Descemet's**\" and \"**Preserves host endothelium**\" — which is precisely what this eye cannot offer, because its Descemet's membrane is the structure that ruptured. The deck's own PKP indication list includes \"scarring, **perforations**, failed DALK\". So the general preference for DALK in keratoconus gives way here to the specific damage. The bank's key and the deck are consistent once the reason is seen. *(The rule that a previous hydrops with a Descemet's break disfavours DALK is **not stated in the course material**; it follows from the deck's own definitions of the two operations. Recorded as an inference.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Continue on the gas permeable contact lens.** | It was working at 6/9 **before** the scar. It cannot correct opacity, and the acuity has already been measured at 6/60 *with correction*. |\n| **Shift to soft contact lenses.** | A step backwards on the very ladder this bank keys twice (`opqb-t8-305`, `opqb-t8-335`) — a soft lens cannot even manage the irregular astigmatism, let alone the scar. |\n| **LASIK.** | **Absolutely contraindicated.** The deck could not be plainer: \"**LASIK is CONTRAINDICATED in keratoconus** · Can cause severe post-op ectasia\", and its red flag, \"Keratoconus-like topography → do NOT operate (LASIK)\". Lasering an already thin, scarred, ectatic cornea would be the worst available decision. |\n\n**What the deck adds about the graft itself:** corneal grafting is \"the most commonly performed and most successful solid organ transplant\", PKP has \"Higher rejection rate; slower visual recovery\" with \"Sutures in situ **12–18 months**\", and the patient must be taught the rejection warning — \"sudden blurred vision, pain, redness, photophobia → **EMERGENCY**\", treated with \"**INTENSIVE topical steroids** (prednisolone acetate 1% hourly)\".\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Hydrops is frightening but self-limiting; it is what it **leaves behind** that decides the operation. Wait for the oedema to clear — as this stem did, over ten weeks — then measure the acuity and look at where the scar sits.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Acute hydrops in keratoconus is a Descemet’s rupture; when the oedema clears it leaves a stromal scar, and a central scar with 6/60 acuity is a keratoplasty — never LASIK.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.51 (Q340), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-341', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'The **earliest indicator** of keratoconus is:',
    options:['Keratometry.','Keratoscopy.','Computerized topography.','Retinoscopy.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Keratometry** – measures **central** corneal curvature; detects advanced or moderate astigmatism, but not early changes\n**Computerized corneal topography** – maps entire corneal surface, **sensitive to very early**, subclinical irregularities\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Keratoconus begins INFERIORLY and off-centre, which is exactly the part of the cornea that keratometry does not sample.** `L15,16) Cornea` names the pattern: \"Topography: **inferior steepening, asymmetric bowtie pattern**\". A keratometer reads a small central ring — roughly 3 mm — so an inferior cone can be well established before it disturbs that ring at all. A topographer maps the whole surface, so it sees the cone while the eye still corrects to 6/6 and the cornea still looks clear (`opqb-t8-334`).\n\n**Why the deck makes this the mandatory screen rather than merely the best test:** \"**Screening for ectasia is MANDATORY before refractive surgery (corneal topography)**\", \"Corneal topography: **mandatory** – asymmetric bowtie, inferior steepening = contraindication\", \"✓ Regular topography pattern\" heading its safe-surgery checklist, and among its top ten messages, \"**Keratoconus screening is mandatory before refractive surgery**\". The entity it is hunting is **forme fruste keratoconus** — subclinical disease that would ectasia catastrophically if lasered.\n\n**Why the other three are later or wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it does, and why it is late |\n|---|---|\n| **Keratometry.** | The bank's own box disposes of it: central curvature only, \"but **not early changes**\". It will eventually show high and irregular readings with distorted mires, but by then the diagnosis is obvious. |\n| **Keratoscopy.** | The Placido disc — concentric rings reflected off the cornea, read **qualitatively** for distortion. It is topography's ancestor and inspects the whole surface, but by eye rather than by computation, so it detects gross irregularity and misses subtle inferior steepening. The second-best answer. *(Keratoscopy is **not named in the course material**; no cached deck or book chapter mentions it. Standard and uncontested.)* |\n| **Retinoscopy.** | A **whole-eye** refraction. It can show the classical **scissoring reflex** of an irregular cornea, which is a genuinely useful early clue at the chair — but it measures the refraction, not the cornea, and it is subjective and non-quantitative. *(The scissoring reflex is likewise **not taken from the course material**.)* |\n\n**What the diagnosis buys.** Early detection is only worth having because there is now something to do with it: **corneal collagen cross-linking**, \"riboflavin (vitamin B2) drops to photosensitize the cornea, then **UV-A** irradiation to strengthen collagen crosslinks\", indicated for \"documented **progressive** keratoconus in young patients\". The deck is careful about what it delivers — \"CXL does **NOT** improve vision – it **stabilizes progression**\" — so the vision it saves is the vision the patient still has when the diagnosis is made. That is the whole argument for finding it early.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Any young patient with rising or unstable astigmatism gets a topography, and no cornea is lasered without one.",
    objective:'Computerised corneal topography is the earliest detector of keratoconus — it maps the whole surface and shows inferior steepening/asymmetric bowtie before keratometry or acuity change.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.51 (Q341), key p.53 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-342', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Corneal edema** is most commonly found with:',
    options:['Corneal scars,','Acute rise of IOP','Megalocornea.','Microcornea'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The cornea stays clear because its endothelium pumps water out of the stroma faster than it leaks in. A sudden rise in intraocular pressure drives water in faster than the pump can remove it, and the cornea clouds.** `L15,16) Cornea` establishes the pump: \"**Endothelium is critical for maintaining transparency via active fluid pumping**\", \"Endothelium: single cell layer, **Na/K ATPase pump** · Cannot regenerate; cell density critical\". Overwhelm it and you get oedema.\n\nAcute angle-closure glaucoma is the clinical case: pressures of 50–70 mmHg produce the epithelial and stromal oedema that makes the cornea look steamy, which is in turn why these patients see **haloes** around lights. The oedema clears as soon as the pressure is brought down — it is a pump that is outmatched, not a pump that is broken. *(The pressure figures and the halo symptom are **not taken from `L15,16) Cornea`**, which teaches the pump but not the glaucoma; acute angle closure is taught in `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry`.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Corneal scars,** | A scar is **fibrous tissue replacing organised lamellae** — \"Damage → **scarring & opacity**\" — with the epithelium healed over it. It is opaque, but it is not oedematous, and it does not fluctuate. Opacity and oedema are different failures of transparency (`opqb-t8-311`). |\n| **Megalocornea.** | An abnormally **large but otherwise normal** cornea. Its clinical importance is that it must be distinguished from **buphthalmos**, where the cornea is large *because* the pressure is high — and it is the buphthalmos, not the megalocornea, that clouds. |\n| **Microcornea.** | An abnormally small cornea. Small, clear and structurally normal. |\n\n**The other routes to corneal oedema**, all of them endothelial and all in the deck:\n\n| Cause | Deck |\n|---|---|\n| **Fuchs' endothelial dystrophy** | \"Abnormal endothelial cell function → **loss of dehydrating pump**\" → guttata → \"Corneal oedema (central stromal → epithelial) → **bullous keratopathy**\", classically \"**worse vision in morning**\" |\n| **Pseudophakic bullous keratopathy** | Surgical endothelial cell loss; a graft indication |\n| **Endothelial cell loss below threshold** | \"Critical density: **<700 cells/mm²** → oedema risk\", from a normal 2,500–3,000 |\n| **Breaks in Descemet's** | Hydrops, buphthalmos — `opqb-t8-330` |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A cloudy cornea in a **painful** eye with a mid-dilated pupil and a hard globe is an acute pressure rise, and it is an emergency — lower the pressure and the cornea clears. A cloudy cornea in a **comfortable** eye that is worst on waking is endothelial dystrophy, and it is a clinic appointment.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Glaucoma — the acute pressure rise itself is taught there; the question tests the corneal consequence.)*",
    objective:'Corneal oedema means the endothelial pump has been outmatched — most commonly by an acute IOP rise; scars are opaque but not oedematous, and megalo/microcornea are clear.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.51 (Q342), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-343', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'In a patient with **herpes zoster ophthalmicus**, the appearance of the following means a **high possibility for corneal affection**:',
    options:['Copious conjunctival discharge.','Massive edema of the eyelids','Eruption on the tip of the nose.','Severe periocular pain.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**This is Hutchinson's sign, and it is an anatomical prediction rather than a clinical impression.** `L15,16) Cornea` states it exactly: \"**Hutchinson's sign: vesicles on tip/side of nose → nasociliary branch involved → HIGH risk of ocular complications**\".\n\n**Why the nose predicts the eye.** The **nasociliary nerve** is a branch of V1, and it divides to supply two territories that look unrelated on the face: the **external nasal branch** reaches the **tip of the nose**, and the **long ciliary nerves** enter the globe to supply the **cornea**. Both are the same nerve. So if the virus has reactivated far enough along the nasociliary branch to raise vesicles on the nose tip, it has by the same token reached the branches that innervate the eye — which is why the sign was worth naming.\n\n**Why the other three do not predict corneal involvement:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Copious conjunctival discharge.** | Discharge is a feature of **bacterial** infection — the deck's bacterial keratitis line reads \"Pain, photophobia, blurred vision, **mucopurulent discharge**\". Zoster is a vesicular, neuralgic disease, and a discharge in this patient would suggest a secondary bacterial infection, not corneal risk. |\n| **Massive edema of the eyelids** | Impressive and common in zoster, and often what brings the patient in — but it reflects the **dermatomal** inflammation and its severity does not track ocular involvement. It follows the skin, not the nerve branch that supplies the cornea. |\n| **Severe periocular pain.** | The rule rather than the exception: the deck names \"**Post-herpetic neuralgia**\" among the complications and \"neuralgic pain\" is in the presentation of `opqb-t8-324`. Present in nearly every case, so it discriminates nothing. |\n\n**What is at stake in the eye**, from the deck's own complication list: \"Epithelial keratitis · Stromal / disciform keratitis · **Neurotrophic keratopathy** · Scleritis / episcleritis · Post-herpetic neuralgia\", together with \"corneal anaesthesia\" as a long-term outcome. The corneal manifestations named are \"punctate epithelial keratitis, **pseudodendritic ulcer**, mucous plaques, disciform keratitis, neurotrophic keratopathy\" — and the zoster **pseudo**dendrite is \"smaller, stellate, **lacks terminal buds**\", which is how it is told from a herpes simplex dendrite.\n\n**What to do about it.** The treatment window is short and is the examinable number: \"Treatment: **oral valaciclovir/famciclovir within 72 h** of rash onset\". Systemic, not topical (`opqb-t8-324` keys the sufficiency of topical acyclovir as **false**). Then examine the eye and test corneal sensation, because the neurotrophic cornea is what loses these eyes years after the rash has faded.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Look at the tip of the nose in every shingles rash of the upper face. It costs one second and it decides whether the patient needs an ophthalmologist.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Hutchinson’s sign — zoster vesicles on the tip or side of the nose — means the nasociliary branch is involved, and the nasociliary nerve also supplies the cornea: high risk of ocular disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.51 (Q343), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-344', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Herpes simplex keratitis** is characterized by the following:',
    options:['It affects primarily the corneal periphery','It shows recurrences.','It does not stain with fluorescein.','Perforation is expected.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Recurrence is the defining behaviour of the disease, because the virus is never cleared — it retreats to the trigeminal ganglion and comes back.** `L15,16) Cornea` treats recurrence as the fact that shapes management: \"**Recurrent episodes**\" is its distinguishing feature in the red-eye differential, and the deck prescribes against it — \"**Prophylactic oral aciclovir 400 mg twice daily reduces recurrence rate by ~50%**\". This bank keys the same fact at `opqb-t8-312` and builds a vignette on it at `opqb-t8-338`.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What is actually true |\n|---|---|\n| **It affects primarily the corneal periphery** | Herpetic disease is characteristically **central or paracentral** — the deck's dendritic-ulcer scenario places the lesion \"in the **central** cornea\", and disciform keratitis is \"**Central** corneal disc-like oedema\". The genuinely **peripheral** corneal diseases are a different group: **marginal keratitis** (\"peripheral subepithelial infiltrates with clear limbal zone\", immune-mediated against staphylococcal antigens), **Mooren's ulcer** and **peripheral ulcerative keratitis**. That centrality is also why herpes costs vision. |\n| **It does not stain with fluorescein.** | The reverse: \"**Fluorescein: stains the ulcer bed**\", with rose bengal staining the virus-laden margins — the two-dye pattern is part of how the diagnosis is made. |\n| **Perforation is expected.** | Not expected; **uncommon**, and largely iatrogenic. The deck attaches it to steroid misuse (\"Can cause geographic ulcer · **Risk of perforation**\") and to the uncommon **necrotizing stromal** form (\"Progressive stromal necrosis & thinning · May lead to perforation · Very difficult to treat\"). |\n\n**⚠️ Sibling with a different key.** `opqb-t8-304` prints almost the same lead-in — \"Herpes simplex keratitis is characterized by\" — with a different option set, and keys **corneal hypoesthesia**. Both recurrence and hypoesthesia are true of the disease; which one is keyed depends entirely on what is offered. This is a bank that reuses stems and changes options, so read the four lines before answering the first.\n\n**What recurrence costs the patient.** Each attack leaves a little more stromal scarring and a little more nerve damage, so over years the eye accumulates opacity **and** anaesthesia — the deck's neurotrophic keratopathy, with HSV heading its cause list. That combination is what turns a treatable epithelial infection into a cornea that needs grafting.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Ask \"has this eye done this before?\". A yes changes the management from a tube of aciclovir to a discussion about long-term prophylaxis — and it makes a steroid drop, from any source, dangerous.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Herpes simplex keratitis recurs (latency in the trigeminal ganglion, halved by prophylactic oral aciclovir), is central rather than peripheral, stains with fluorescein, and rarely perforates.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.51 (Q344), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-345', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'**Ultrasonography** is **not indicated** in:',
    options:['Mature senile cataract.','Vitreous hemorrhage.','Dense corneal scar.','Glaucomatous optic atrophy'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Ocular B-scan exists to see past media you cannot see through. In glaucomatous optic atrophy the media are perfectly clear — you can look straight at the disc — so the ultrasound has nothing to add.** The other three options are all opaque media, and each blocks the view of the posterior segment at a different level: the **cornea** (a dense scar), the **lens** (a mature cataract) and the **vitreous** (a haemorrhage).\n\n| Option | What is opaque | What you are looking for behind it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mature senile cataract.** | The lens | Retinal detachment, tumour, staphyloma — before committing to surgery, since a white lens hides everything |\n| **Vitreous hemorrhage.** | The vitreous | A **retinal tear or detachment** underlying the bleed — the finding that changes management from observation to urgent surgery |\n| **Dense corneal scar.** | The cornea | The state of the posterior segment before offering a keratoplasty; the deck's own graft contraindication is \"**Poor visual potential** (retinal/optic nerve disease)\", and a B-scan is one way to establish it |\n| **Glaucomatous optic atrophy** | **Nothing** | The disc is visible on **fundoscopy**; the disease is assessed by cup:disc ratio, **visual fields**, IOP and **OCT** of the nerve fibre layer |\n\n**What the material says about ocular ultrasound.** The cornea deck uses ultrasound only in trauma, and for a different purpose — \"Corneal foreign body: … check for retained **intraocular foreign body (CT scan)**\". The module's printed B-scan indication is in the sclera material: `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 37, posterior scleritis, \"**U/S: Scleral thickening ± RD**\" with the \"**T sign**\" figure; and book ch.7 p.100, \"**Ocular B-scan ultrasonography** and orbital **magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)** may be used for the detection of posterior scleritis\". *(The general principle — B-scan is the investigation for opaque media — is **not printed as a rule** in any cached ophthalmology deck or book chapter. It is standard and uncontested, and it is what the option list is built on. Recorded as outside knowledge.)*\n\n**Why option D is nonetheless the hardest of the four.** Ultrasound is not entirely absent from the optic nerve: **optic nerve sheath diameter** on B-scan is used to assess raised intracranial pressure, and **optic disc drusen** are classically identified by their acoustic reflectivity. Neither is glaucomatous atrophy, and neither is offered.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Ask one question before ordering an ocular ultrasound: *can I see in?* If you can, look — the examination is better than the scan. If you cannot, scan, and say which layer is blocking you, because that determines what you are looking for.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapters: Glaucoma — the disease named in the keyed option — and Cataract; the question tests B-scan indications rather than any corneal fact, and is filed here because the bank prints it in the Cornea topic and the corneal-scar option is its corneal link.)*",
    objective:'Ocular B-scan is for opaque media — dense corneal scar, mature cataract, vitreous haemorrhage; glaucomatous optic atrophy has clear media and is assessed by fundoscopy, fields, IOP and OCT.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.51 (Q345), key p.53' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-346', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'A **68-year-old male** comes for a routine eye check-up. On slit-lamp examination, you notice a **bilateral white-gray ring at the peripheral cornea with a clear zone separating it from the limbus**. He has **no visual complaints**. Which of the following is true regarding it?',
    options:['Requires surgical removal','Usually asymptomatic','Causes severe visual impairment','Mostly Unilateral'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Classic description: **Arcus senilis**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim — the bank names the entity and leaves the reasoning to the reader. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Arcus senilis is lipid deposited in the peripheral corneal stroma. It sits outside the visual axis, it is bilateral, it is a normal ageing change after about 60, and it does nothing to vision — so the only true statement here is that it is asymptomatic.** `L15,16) Cornea` files it under age-related degenerations: \"**Arcus senilis (lipid arc at limbus – physiological in elderly, check lipids if <45 yrs)**\".\n\n**Every clause of the stem is part of the description:**\n\n| Stem detail | Why it fits arcus |\n|---|---|\n| **Bilateral** | Deposition is a systemic lipid phenomenon, so both eyes are affected. Strictly unilateral arcus is the exception that suggests **carotid disease on the opposite side** — reduced perfusion protects that cornea. |\n| **Peripheral, white-gray ring** | Lipid in the peripheral stroma, denser superiorly and inferiorly first, later closing into a full ring. |\n| **Clear zone separating it from the limbus** | The **lucid interval of Vogt** — the diagnostic feature, and what distinguishes arcus from a **limbal girdle** or from peripheral inflammatory disease. |\n| **No visual complaints** | It never reaches the visual axis. |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why |\n|---|---|\n| **Requires surgical removal** | There is no treatment and none is needed. Nothing in the deck's degeneration list attaches an operation to arcus — the degenerations that do get treated are **band keratopathy** (\"EDTA chelation… or excimer laser PTK\") and **pterygium** (\"surgical excision **if affecting vision or inducing astigmatism**\"). |\n| **Causes severe visual impairment** | It is peripheral by definition; a lesion that spared the axis cannot impair acuity. |\n| **Mostly Unilateral** | The reverse — and, as above, unilateral arcus is itself a red flag rather than the norm. |\n\n**The one thing that makes it worth mentioning at all: the AGE.** The deck's parenthesis is an instruction — \"**check lipids if <45 yrs**\". In a patient under about 45 the same ring is called **arcus juvenilis** and may signal familial hyperlipidaemia, so it earns a fasting lipid profile and a cardiovascular assessment. At **68**, as here, it is expected and needs nothing. *(The lucid interval of Vogt, the unilateral-arcus carotid association and the term arcus juvenilis are **not taken from the course material** — the deck gives the entity, the substance, the site and the age rule, and no more. They are standard and uncontested.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Recognise it, name it, do not investigate it — unless the patient is young, in which case the eye has just made a cardiovascular diagnosis for you.",
    objective:'Arcus senilis is peripheral stromal lipid with a clear zone at the limbus: bilateral, asymptomatic, untreated and physiological after ~60 — but under 45 it demands a lipid profile.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.51 (Q346), key p.53 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-347', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'A **72-year-old woman with Parkinson’s disease** presents with foreign body sensation, tearing, and **intermittent blurry vision, especially in the morning**. On examination, **punctate epithelial erosions are noted in the inferior cornea**. What is the most likely underlying cause of her corneal findings?',
    options:['Lagophthalmos due to incomplete eyelid closure','Herpes simplex keratitis','Acute angle-closure glaucoma','Corneal dystrophy'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Parkinson’s disease** reduced blink rate, incomplete eyelid closure (**lagophthalmos**) **Inferior** corneal punctate epithelial erosions, especially **worse in the morning**, suggest **exposure keratopathy**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Two features fix the mechanism as exposure: the erosions are INFERIOR, and the symptoms are worst in the MORNING.** `L15,16) Cornea` defines exposure keratopathy in the same terms: \"**Incomplete lid closure → inferior epithelial breakdown** · Causes: VII nerve palsy (Bell's), thyroid eye disease, proptosis · Treatment: **lubricants, taping eyelid, moisture chamber, tarsorrhaphy**\". Inferior, because that is the strip the lid fails to cover; worst on waking, because the eye has been drying all night with nobody blinking.\n\n**Why Parkinson's disease does this.** Bradykinesia affects the blink as it affects every other movement: the blink rate falls from around 15 per minute to a fraction of that, and the blinks that do occur are often incomplete, so the tear film is neither renewed nor spread. Add nocturnal lagophthalmos and the inferior cornea dries out. *(Parkinson's disease is **not on `L15,16) Cornea`'s printed cause list** for exposure keratopathy — that list is VII palsy, thyroid eye disease and proptosis. The reduced-blink mechanism is standard and uncontested and is **not taken from the course material**; the bank's own box supplies it.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Herpes simplex keratitis** | Would give a **discrete branching dendrite** with terminal buds, usually **unilateral**, painful and photophobic, with ciliary injection and recurrent attacks — not a bilateral field of fine inferior dots that is worst on waking. |\n| **Acute angle-closure glaucoma** | An emergency of hours: severe pain, haloes, nausea, a **steamy** cornea from oedema, a mid-dilated fixed pupil and a hard globe. Nothing here is acute and nothing is painful in that sense. |\n| **Corneal dystrophy** | The deck's dystrophies are \"bilateral, primarily non-inflammatory, **heritable**, progressive opacifying disorders\", presenting decades earlier and without a systemic explanation. The trap is **Fuchs' endothelial dystrophy**, which also gives \"**worse vision in morning**\" — but for the opposite reason: overnight the eye cannot evaporate its **oedema**, so the whole cornea is hazy and the vision **improves through the day**. Fuchs' clouds the cornea; exposure erodes its inferior epithelium. Read *where* the signs are. |\n\n**What to do.** Nothing here needs a specialist first: preservative-free lubricants through the day, **ointment and lid taping at night**, a moisture chamber if that is not enough, and a **tarsorrhaphy** if the cornea keeps breaking down — the deck's own ladder. The danger of leaving it is the same as in any exposure state: a persistent epithelial defect that ulcerates and melts.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In any patient with a neurological disease that slows or weakens the face — Parkinson's, Bell's palsy, coma, sedation, thyroid eye disease — ask specifically whether the eyes close at night, and look at the **inferior third** of the cornea.",
    objective:'Reduced blink and incomplete lid closure give exposure keratopathy: INFERIOR punctate erosions worst in the morning — treated with lubricants, night ointment, taping and tarsorrhaphy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.51 (Q347), key p.53 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t8-348', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'A **25-year-old welder** presents with **intense bilateral eye pain, photophobia, tearing, and a foreign body sensation several hours after working without protective goggles.** Visual acuity is mildly reduced. Fluorescein staining reveals **diffuse punctate epithelial erosions.** Which of the following is **NOT recommended** in the treatment of this case?',
    options:['Artificial tears','Oral analgesics','Topical anesthetic drops','Ointments for lubrication'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Topical anesthetic drops** contraindicated for home use because they **delay epithelial healing and may cause corneal toxicity**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**`L15,16) Cornea` bans them twice, and once in capitals.** In its *Common Prescribing Errors* table, under **Topical Anaesthetics for Analgesia**: \"**Inhibit epithelial healing · Cause stromal melting · Anaesthetic keratopathy → perforation · NEVER prescribe for prolonged outpatient use**\". In its contact-lens scenario: \"**NEVER prescribe topical anaesthetics for outpatient analgesia**\". And among its top ten take-home messages: \"**Never prescribe topical anaesthetics for outpatient analgesia**\".\n\n**Why the ban is absolute rather than cautious.** The drop works — that is the problem. It abolishes the pain instantly, so the patient uses it again, and each dose does three things: it poisons the epithelial cells that are trying to migrate across the defect, so the defect will not close; it anaesthetises the cornea, producing exactly the **neurotrophic** state described at `opqb-t8-298` in which the eye no longer protects itself; and it removes the pain that would otherwise have brought the patient back. The end point in the deck's own words is **stromal melting and perforation**. A cornea can be lost this way in days.\n\n**The diagnosis, for completeness:** this is **ultraviolet photokeratitis** — arc eye, welder's flash, snow blindness. UV-B from the arc kills the superficial epithelial cells; they slough some hours later, which is why the pain begins **several hours** after the exposure rather than at the time, and why it is **bilateral** and diffusely punctate. It is self-limiting and heals in 24–48 hours, in line with the deck's abrasion timescale: \"usually heals in **24–48 h**\". *(The UV mechanism and the eponyms are **not taken from the course material** — `L15,16)` teaches chemical and mechanical trauma but no radiation injury; measured, *photokeratitis*, *arc eye* and *welder* return no hit in any cached ophthalmology deck or book chapter. Standard and uncontested.)*\n\n**Why the other three are correct treatment:**\n\n| Option | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **Artificial tears** | Surface protection and comfort while the epithelium regenerates. |\n| **Oral analgesics** | The **right** way to treat this pain — systemic, not topical. Severe cases may also need a cycloplegic for the ciliary spasm (`opqb-t8-307`). |\n| **Ointments for lubrication** | Longer contact time, particularly overnight; a topical antibiotic ointment doubles as prophylaxis over a large bare area. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Diagnose it, reassure the patient it will be over by tomorrow, give oral analgesia and lubrication — and, most importantly, do not hand over the bottle that made the examination bearable. Then talk about the goggles.\n\n*(⚠️ This question is printed in the LEFT column of book p.52, the same page whose right column begins the Cornea answers.)*",
    objective:'UV photokeratitis (welder’s flash) is self-limiting in 24–48 h and treated with lubricants and ORAL analgesia; topical anaesthetics are never dispensed — they block healing and cause melting.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.52 (Q348), key p.53 (explanation box printed)' }








